2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.011 134.4KB 137.95s
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] No explicit question was attached, so here are a few review notes from reading through both files.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] I didn’t run them; these are static observations.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] • assembler.ts*
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - It’s a parser/label tracker so far, not yet a code generator. `ram` is allocated but never
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] written to, and the assembled bytes column is hard-coded to `00 00 00`.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Any line that doesn’t start with a label is misparsed. `ORG 8000` and `LDA #00` will treat the
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] first word as a label and then read the opcode from the wrong position.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Operand length decisions use truthiness: `00` and `0000` are treated as “no operand”, which will
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] produce wrong instruction sizes.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Forward label references are all treated as 3-byte absolute references, but branch targets should
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] be 2-byte relative offsets. There is no separate mode for relative branches.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Only lowercase `,x` and `),y` are handled. Uppercase suffixes like `,X` and `),Y` fall through to
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] label handling.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `($nn,X)` indirect addressing is not implemented.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `($nnnn),Y` is incorrectly treated as absolute-Y; on real 6502 this mode uses a zero-page
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] pointer.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] • machine.ts*
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - JSR/RTS appears to be off by one. JSR pushes `pc+2` and RTS adds another 1, so the return address
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] becomes `pc+3` instead of `pc+2`. On 6502, JSR pushes `return-1`, and RTS adds 1 after pulling.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `machineRom` is set to `plus4`, but `isC64` is true. Plus/4 hardware uses TED rather than
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] VIC/CIA, so the C64 I/O handling may not match the selected ROM unless this is intentional.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The interpreter is a large handwritten switch. Many valid opcodes aren’t implemented yet, so any
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] program that hits one will stop with “unsupported opcode”.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The static opcode table contains `mode` fields but the interpreter mostly ignores them. Decoding
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] via that table would reduce the risk of switch-case drift.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - C64 color RAM at `$D800` is written to memory, but the page-based switch in `writeAddress`
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] doesn’t handle it specially and logs it as unknown.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `readCIA` just returns raw memory, so CIA timers/keyboard input aren’t really emulated yet.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] Overall the direction is clear, but both files look like work-in-progress scaffolding rather than
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] complete implementations. Let me know if you’d like me to focus on a specific fix or refactor.
2026-08-01 15:04:56 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
