2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] A few fixes to your code:
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `loadNib` is `async`, so you need `await`:
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash]   const tracks = await loadNib("applepanic.nib");
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `for (let track in tracks)` iterates index strings, not `track` objects. Use:
2026-08-01 16:48:27 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]   for (const track of tracks) {
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]     console.log(track.view.byteLength);
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]   }
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `interface track` – conventional naming is `Track` (capitalized), but that’s cosmetic.
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] - If the file isn’t exactly `35 * 6656` bytes, the loop will silently create malformed `DataView`s.
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] Add a length check:
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]   const expected = 35 * 6656;
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]   if (buffer.byteLength !== expected) {
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]     console.warn(`[NIB] expected ${expected} bytes, got ${buffer.byteLength}`);
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash]   }
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] Also, standard .nib tracks are already in 6-and-2 nibblized form, so you can feed each 6656-byte
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] track straight into your disk nibble reader — no extra decode step. That’s exactly the "simpler"
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] path.
2026-08-01 16:48:28 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:54:25 [nitro@ryzen5] 6656, so it is effectively circular in nature?
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 32.57KB 12.86s
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes — a physical disk track is a continuous ring of bits, and the `.nib` track of 6,656 bytes
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] stores that entire ring as a fixed-size circular buffer.
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] So:
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Byte 0 and byte 6,655 are physically adjacent on the disk.
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] - When the drive reads a sector, it doesn't start at a fixed "track beginning"; it starts wherever
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] the read head happens to be. The nibble stream just wraps around.
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Your nibble reader should use modulo indexing:
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash]   const nibble = trackNibbles[(offset + position) % 6656];
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash]   or wrap manually when `position` reaches 6656.
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] The `6656` count itself is 16 sectors × 416 raw nibbles per sector — that's the full formatted
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] ring, so it lines up nicely.
2026-08-01 16:54:38 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:13 [nitro@ryzen5] so is should detext start and then decode?
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 33.51KB 18.39s
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes, exactly. Because the track is circular, you can't just read from byte 0. You need to scan
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] for a start marker — the address prologue `D5 AA 96` (and then the data prologue `D5 AA AD`) —
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] and then decode the bytes that follow.
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] Here's the typical approach:
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Search the 6656-byte track for `D5 AA 96`.
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash]    When found, the next 3 bytes form the address field (track, sector, volume, checksum).
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash]    That tells you which sector you're at.
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Then search for `D5 AA AD` (the data prologue).
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash]    After that, the next 342 nibbles are the 6-and-2 encoded data for that sector.
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Decode those 342 nibbles with your existing `decodeDos33Sector()` (which already does
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] the 6-and-2 → 256-byte decompression).
2026-08-01 16:55:32 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. Because it's circular, when you read past byte 6655 you wrap to byte 0.
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash]    You can either:
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - use `% 6656` indexing, or
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash]    - copy the track twice into a 13312-byte buffer and search there (many emulators do this).
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash] So yes — detect start, then decode. That's the standard DOS 3.3 nibble-reading flow.
2026-08-01 16:55:33 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
