2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 36.38KB 65.60s
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] Only track 2 showing `D5 AA 96` is a strong sign that the disk is not uniformly standard DOS
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3.3 — very common with copy-protected games like Apple Panic. It’s also possible the image
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] isn’t actually a nibblized `.nib` at all.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] First, check what’s actually in each track. Add a quick inspection pass:
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] for (let t = 0; t < tracks.length; t++) {
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 	const bytes = new Uint8Array(tracks[t].view.buffer, tracks[t].view.byteOffset, 6656);
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 	const d5 = bytes.filter(b => b === 0xD5).length;
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 	const d5aa = bytes.filter((_, i) => bytes[i] === 0xD5 && bytes[(i + 1) % 6656] === 0xAA).length;
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 	const addr = bytes.filter((_, i) => bytes[i] === 0xD5 && bytes[(i + 1) % 6656] === 0xAA && bytes[(i + 2) % 6656] === 0x96).length;
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 	const data = bytes.filter((_, i) => bytes[i] === 0xD5 && bytes[(i + 1) % 6656] === 0xAA && bytes[(i + 2) % 6656] === 0xAD).length;
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 	console.log(t, "D5:", d5, "D5AA:", d5aa, "ADDR:", addr, "DATA:", data, "FIRST:", bytes.slice(0, 8).map(b => b.toString(16).padStart(2, "0")).join(" "));
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] What to look for:
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Normal DOS 3.3 track: roughly 16 `ADDR` and 16 `DATA` marks per track.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Track 2 only: copy protection / custom track formatting.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Almost no `D5` at all: the file may not be nibblized. A `.dsk` or decoded sector image
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] will look like raw 6502 bytes, not `D5/AA/96/AD` markers. Track 2’s hit may just be a coincidence.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Many `D5AA` but third byte isn’t `96`: protected tracks use alternate address prologues.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] You can search for `D5 AA` and print the third byte to see what the disk actually uses.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] Also verify file size:
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] console.log("file bytes:", buffer.byteLength, "expected:", 35 * 6656);
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] If that doesn’t match, the track slicing is wrong and everything after the first track will be
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] garbage.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] For a real copy-protected `.nib`, scanning for standard markers isn’t enough — you may need to
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] identify each track’s actual prologue, then decode 6-and-2 from there. If the tracks are truly
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] non-standard (or bit-slipped), the simpler path is to fall back to a WOZ image or use your
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] `loadwoz.ts` disk element emulation instead.
2026-08-01 17:03:26 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
