2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 62.35KB 14.09s
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] Good progress — the address field offsets are now correct (`volume, track, sector, crc`). That
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] should give you proper values in the log.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] A few remaining issues to be aware of:
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Sector count is now 32 per track, not 16.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash]    You push `i` for address prologues and `-i` for data prologues, so `sectors.length` counts both.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want the real sector count, count only non-negative entries:
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash]    const addressCount = sectors.filter(v => v >= 0).length;
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash]    or just keep separate arrays.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Decoded sectors are not linked to their track/sector numbers.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash]    You push the raw decoded 256 bytes into `blocks`, but you don't know which track/sector they
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] belong to. The data prologue alone doesn't tell you; you need to match it with the preceding
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] address prologue (the closest `D5 AA 96` before it).
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. The checksum nibble is not read.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash]    After the 342 data nibbles, DOS 3.3 stores a checksum value (at `dataStart + 342`) and then `DE
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] AA EB`. You don't have to verify it for loading, but if you plan to write sectors later you'll need
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] to generate it.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. The global `temp` array is reused across calls.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash]    That's fine for single-threaded use, but be careful: if `decodeDos33Sector` fails partway, stale
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] data from the previous sector remains in `temp[0..k-1]`. Local allocation is safer.
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] Next step: build a `decodeTrack` that returns `{ track, sector, bytes }` for each sector, then
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] store them into a `disk[track][sector]` array. Once you have that, you can mount the result in your
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] Disk II emulation and actually boot the game. Want help wiring that up?
2026-08-01 18:21:04 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:35 [nitro@ryzen5] pass is mostly false
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 68.64KB 10.78s
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] `pass` is mostly false because you're checking for the wrong epilogue.
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] After the 342 data nibbles and the checksum byte, a DOS 3.3 data field ends with:
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] DE AA EB
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] not `D5 AA 96`. `D5 AA 96` is the address prologue that begins the next sector, and
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] usually there are several gap bytes between the end of one field and the start of the next. So
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] checking `bytes[dataStart + 343..345]` for `D5 AA 96` will almost always fail.
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] Correct check:
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] const epilogue1 = bytes[(dataStart + 343) % n];
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] const epilogue2 = bytes[(dataStart + 344) % n];
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] const epilogue3 = bytes[(dataStart + 345) % n];
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] const pass = epilogue1 === 0xDE && epilogue2 === 0xAA && epilogue3 === 0xEB;
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] You can also verify the checksum itself: it should equal the XOR of all 342 decoded 6-bit values
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] (i.e., `temp[0..341]` before merging into 256 bytes). That’s a stronger test than the epilogue.
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want to see `pass` true consistently, use the epilogue check above. If you still see
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] failures, the track may be copy-protected or the sector layout is non-standard.
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] The bigger task is still linking each decoded block to its `track`/`sector`, and building a
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] `disk[track][sector]` array. Once that’s done, you can mount it in the Disk II emulation.
2026-08-01 18:26:49 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:27 [nitro@ryzen5] thanks!
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.004 74.58KB 7.81s
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] Good — with `DE AA EB` as the epilogue, `pass` should now be true for every data field on a
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] standard DOS 3.3 track.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] The remaining piece is that `detectSectors` collects decoded blocks but never links them to their
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] `track`/`sector` numbers. Right now the address fields are logged, and the data fields are decoded,
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] but the two aren't paired. To build a usable disk image you'll want to:
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Scan for an address prologue, read `track`/`sector`.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Find the next data prologue after it (search forward up to ~600 bytes, wrapping).
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Decode those 342 nibbles into 256 bytes.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. Store into `disk[track][sector]`.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] That's the key integration step for mounting the disk in your Disk II emulation.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] Also, if you want to be stricter, verify the checksum after decoding — it's just XOR of all 342
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] decoded 6-bit values before the 6-and-2 merge. But for loading, it's optional.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] One more small thing: `sectors` mixes positive (address offsets) and negative (data offsets), so
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] the count is 32 per track. If you ever want to display "16 sectors", filter for `>= 0`.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] Nice progress — you're one step away from booting `RESCUERAIDERS.nib` in the emulator.
2026-08-01 18:27:39 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
