2026-08-03 11:10:10 [nitro@ryzen5] when the vic20 flashed its cursor what underlying effect does it use?
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.000 3.605KB 8.98s
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] The VIC‑20’s cursor is generated by the VIC chip (6560/6561) directly. It’s not a character
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] that the CPU changes; it’s a separate overlay.
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The VIC chip has a cursor enable bit that, when set, displays a solid block at the
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] current screen position.
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The chip automatically toggles that bit on/off every frame (or every other frame
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] depending on the chip revision), producing the blink — the CPU does nothing.
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The KERNAL ROM only manages where the cursor is (the screen pointer) and turns it on/off with
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] routines like `CHROUT` or `CURS_ON`/`CURS_OFF`.
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] So the effect is a hardware‑generated, blinking overlay block, not a software‑drawn
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] character inversion or color change. That’s why the cursor blinks smoothly without any CPU
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] overhead.
2026-08-03 11:10:20 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
