2026-08-03 11:11:15 [nitro@ryzen5] vic20 flashes its cursor, how? bg color or charmap?
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 4.986KB 2.74s
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] Neither — it's hardware blinking in the VIC chip itself.
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] The VIC-20's cursor is generated directly by the VIC chip (6560/6561), not by changing the screen
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] RAM or character ROM. When cursor is enabled, the chip:
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:11:18 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Draws a solid block at the cursor position each frame
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Toggles visibility automatically (on/off rhythm) without CPU intervention
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] - Uses the chip's internal timing, independent of any character data
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] So it's pure hardware overlay — the VIC chip just flips a signal to show/hide that block
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] repeatedly. No background color tricks, no character swaps. The CPU only tells it where to
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] draw and whether to enable it.
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] That's the elegant part: the blink is "free" — zero CPU cycles wasted on it.
2026-08-03 11:11:19 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:11:57 [nitro@ryzen5] how does keyboard sensing work
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.002 5.952KB 3.58s
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] The VIC-20's keyboard is a 6x7 matrix scanned by the CIA1 chip (6526).
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] • How it works:*
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 1. Matrix layout: Keys are arranged in rows and columns. The CIA1 outputs to one row at a
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] time (via Port A), and reads back which columns have a key pressed (via Port B).
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 2. Software polling: The KERNAL ROM repeatedly:
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Sets one row line low (active low)
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Reads the column lines
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - If a column reads low, that key is pressed
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Moves to the next row, repeats
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 3. Debouncing: The ROM waits a bit between reads to filter out bouncing contacts.
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:12:01 [claude-haiku-4-5] 4. Interrupt option: CIA1 can also generate an interrupt when any key is pressed
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] (flag bit), so the CPU doesn't have to poll constantly — but early VIC-20 code often just polls
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] anyway.
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] • Result*: The CPU discovers which key is down by testing each row/column combination until
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] it finds a low signal. It's simple matrix scanning with no dedicated keyboard controller — the CIA1
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] just provides the I/O lines.
2026-08-03 11:12:02 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 12:05:26 [claude-haiku-4-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.037 254.0KB 11.91s
2026-08-03 12:05:26 [claude-haiku-4-5] Looking at your machine.ts - solid 6502 emulator foundation. A few observations:
2026-08-03 12:05:26 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
