2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] On real C64, the cursor is not a VIC chip hardware feature—it's entirely
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] software-managed.
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] • How it actually works:*
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 1. Character replacement: The cursor is displayed by writing a specific character code to
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] screen RAM at the cursor position
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Typically A0 (reverse video space) or 7F (underscore-like glyph)
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Or a custom character from the character ROM
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 2. Blinking via software:
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - The BASIC interpreter (or application) polls a timer and periodically writes/erases the cursor
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] character
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Uses a counter in zero-page RAM (typically around A2-A4 area in Commodore BASIC)
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5]    - Every N frames, it XORs or toggles the character at cursor position
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 3. No VIC chip involvement: The VIC-II doesn't track cursor state—it just renders whatever
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] character code is in screen RAM at that location
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] • In your emulator context:*
2026-08-03 13:37:35 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
