2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.003 8.469KB 3.03s
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] Yes, `MIM_DATA` is needed if you only want to process actual MIDI data messages.
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] The callback receives different message types. `MIM_DATA` specifically means "MIDI input data
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] arrived". Other types include:
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] - `MIM_OPEN` — device opened
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] - `MIM_CLOSE` — device closed
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] - `MIM_ERROR` — invalid MIDI data
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] - `MIM_LONGDATA` — system exclusive (sysex) messages
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] Without the check, you'd process all callback invocations, including device lifecycle events that
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] don't contain valid MIDI data in `_dwParam1`.
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] • If you only care about MIDI data*, keep the check. If you need to handle other events
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] (like sysex), add more conditions:
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] if (msgType === MIM_DATA) {
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	// handle note on/off, CC, etc.
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] } else if (msgType === MIM_LONGDATA) {
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 	// handle sysex
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] }
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] So yes — the `MIM_DATA` check is the right approach here.
2026-08-04 18:49:45 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:28 [nitro@ryzen5] in ts can i empty a const record?
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-haiku-4-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.001 502B 3.80s
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] No, you can't empty a `const` record in TypeScript in the traditional sense. `const` prevents
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] reassignment, but it doesn't prevent mutation of the record's contents.
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] What you can do:
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] const record: Record<string, string> = { a: "1", b: "2" };
2026-08-04 19:38:32 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] // Delete properties
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] delete record.a;
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] delete record.b;
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] // Or clear all properties
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] Object.keys(record).forEach(key => delete record[key]);
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] What you can't do:
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] const record: Record<string, string> = { a: "1" };
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] record = {}; // ❌ Error: Cannot assign to const
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] If you need true immutability or want to create a new empty record, use `let` instead:
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] let record: Record<string, string> = { a: "1", b: "2" };
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] record = {}; // ✓ Works
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] What's your use case? If you're trying to clear data but keep the variable reference, deleting
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] properties works. If you want to reassign, switch to `let`.
2026-08-04 19:38:33 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
