2026-08-05 17:32:24 [claude-haiku-4-5] FFFX Addresses - 6502 Interrupt Vectors
2026-08-05 17:32:24 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:24 [claude-haiku-4-5] The $FFFx region contains the 6502 processor's interrupt vector table at the top of memory:
2026-08-05 17:32:24 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:24 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:24 [claude-haiku-4-5] $FFFA-$FFFB  NMI vector    (Non-Maskable Interrupt)
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] $FFFC-$FFFD  RESET vector  (Power-on / Reset)
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] $FFFE-$FFFF  IRQ vector    (Interrupt Request)
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] Each is a 16-bit little-endian address (low byte first).
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] In machine.ts
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] You're setting these vectors during `reset()`:
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] this.memory[0xFFFA] = nmi & 0xff;      // NMI low
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] this.memory[0xFFFB] = nmi >> 8;        // NMI high
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] this.memory[0xFFFC] = reset & 0xff;    // RESET low
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] this.memory[0xFFFD] = reset >> 8;      // RESET high
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] this.memory[0xFFFE] = irq & 0xff;      // IRQ low
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] this.memory[0xFFFF] = irq >> 8;        // IRQ high
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] In notes.txt
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] The trace shows TED reading/writing these addresses during initialization—the Plus/4 ROM is
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] configuring its interrupt handlers at startup. The pattern suggests the ROM is copying or verifying
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] handler addresses into RAM before enabling interrupts.
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] 
2026-08-05 17:32:25 [claude-haiku-4-5] The values shown (`FFA4`, `F2F6`, etc.) are the actual handler entry points in the Plus/4 ROM.
