2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] Thanks for sharing — the notes.txt error has a clear cause, and reading through `assembler.ts`
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] there are a few related issues queued up right behind it.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] The immediate error
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] `parse6502` only has switch cases for `Mode.Absolute` and `Mode.None`. Every other mode — Immediate
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] (1), Zeropage (2), indexed, branch, indirect — falls into `default` and logs "unsupported
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] addressing mode". Your log entry is `AND #10`: mode 1 is `Mode.Immediate`, ref8 16 is `10`. So this
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] isn't a bad operand parse; the emit logic simply isn't written yet.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] Issues that will surface as you implement it
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - Enum order doesn't match opcode order. In `mos6502.json`, AND's opcodes `29 25 35 2D 3D
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 39 21 31` follow the order imm, zp, zpX, abs, absX, absY, (ind,X), (ind),Y — but `Mode.Immediate`
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] is 1, `Mode.Zeropage` is 2, etc. You'll need a per-spec lookup from `Mode` to opcode index rather
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] than using the enum value directly. `profile[spec].bytes[i]` then gives you the instruction length
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] for free, which would tidy up the manual `bytes=1/2/3` logic in the main loop.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - `Mode.Indirect8` / `Mode.Indirect16` don't exist. `indirect()` returns these, but the
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] enum has no such members, so those paths yield `mode: undefined` at runtime. Either add them or map
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] onto the existing entries.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - Missing `break` after `case 5:` in the `if(mode==11)` block — branches fall through and
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] get overwritten to `Mode.Absolute`. Also worth noting spec 8 (JMP) isn't handled there at all, so
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] `JMP NEXTITEM` (which WozMon uses) currently lands in default too.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - `(zp,X)` operands are being silently misclassified. `addressMode` only checks
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] `endsWith("),y")` and `endsWith(")")` for parenthesised operands. `(STL,X)` and `(XAML,X)` match
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] the plain `")"` branch, the slice becomes `"STL,X"`, and `findLabel` then auto-creates a bogus
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] label named `STL,X`. WozMon uses both forms, so this one matters.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - Case sensitivity: the `",x"` / `"),y"` tests only match lowercase, while the canonical
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] WozMon source uses `,X`. Normalising the operand once at the top of `addressMode` would prevent a
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] class of silent misses.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - `hex16(operands, lineNumber)` in the Absolute case looks the operand up as a label, so a
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] raw `$FF00` operand resolves to -1 and logs "bad address". `code.ref16` already holds the parsed
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] value — using it directly avoids the double parse.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] - Dead code: `detectMode` is never called, and its parenthesised-operand logic conflicts
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] with `addressMode` anyway. Probably worth deleting to avoid confusion.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] For branches, remember the emitted byte is `target − (address + 2)`, which means labels need
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] pass-one resolution before pass two emits — your two-pass structure already supports that.
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] Happy to write the opcode-index lookup and the missing switch cases if you'd like — just say the
2026-08-07 10:13:36 [kimi-k3] word.
