2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] The notes.txt sample is a nice capture — it shows two distinct bugs in one glance, and tracing them
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] back through assembler.ts is instructive.
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] • 1. Opcode table indexing (the `99` that should be `9D`)*
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] `decode6502` hardcodes opcode indexes that only line up with the 8-opcode layout of the spec-15
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] instructions (LDA, ADC, etc.). For anything with a shorter opcode list the indexes drift:
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] - STA has no immediate mode, so its list is `85 95 8D 9D 99 81 91`. The `,X` absolute path uses
2026-08-07 18:19:31 [kimi-k3] `lookOp(opcodes,4)`, which lands on `99` (abs,Y) instead of index 3 (`9D`, abs,X). That's exactly
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] what your sample shows.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - Same class of error elsewhere: CPX/CPY absolute reads past the end of a 3-entry list and returns
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] an empty opcode; BIT zero-page picks up `2C`; STX/STY zero-page indexed picks up the absolute
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] opcode; LDX `,Y` 16-bit indexes out of range.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - Indirect modes are also off: `(zp),Y` uses index 2 (which is zp,X for spec-15 ops) instead of 7,
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] and `(zp,X)` similarly. JMP indirect (`6C`) isn't reachable at all.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] The `spec` field in mos6502.json looks like it was intended to drive this, but nothing reads it
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] during decoding. Until the index math is derived from the spec (or the opcodes are keyed by mode
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] name), only the spec-15 instructions and spec-12 shifts assemble correctly.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] • 2. Operand resolution (the `FF FFFF`)*
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] Two compounding issues:
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - `L-1` is treated as a literal label name. `findLabel` auto-creates it with value 0, and since
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] nothing ever defines it, it stays 0. There's no expression handling for label±offset, which
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] wozmon-style sources use heavily.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - `labelMap.get(label) || -1` turns a legitimate address of 0 into -1 (0 is falsy). Then `hex(-1 >>
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 8)` masks to 0xffff and padStart(2) yields four characters — hence the three "bytes". `??` or an
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] explicit `has()` check would at least have produced `00 00`, making the unresolved symbol visible
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] rather than corrupting the column width. Same pattern in `byteReference`.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] • 3. Branch offset sign*
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] `decodeBranch` computes `(currentAddress + 2 - target) & 255` — that's the negated offset. The 6502
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] displacement is `target - (pc + 2)`, so every taken branch lands the wrong direction. Also,
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] `if(target)` treats a not-found label (-1, truthy) as valid and computes a delta against it.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] • 4. `decodeJump` will throw*
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] It references `slice.slice(1,5)`, but the parameter is named `operand` and there's no `slice` in
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] scope — any `$`-prefixed JSR/JMP target is a runtime ReferenceError.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] • 5. Data directives don't advance the address*
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] `.asciiz` and `.word` build a `bytes` string that's never consumed, `.byte` only logs, and after
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] the switch `pos += n` adds the file's line count to the column pointer (harmless only
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] because nothing reads `pos` afterward). Net effect: `currentAddress` doesn't move across data, so
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] every label and the branch math after the first data block in commodoreplus.txt will be skewed.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] • 6. Smaller items worth noting*
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - Zero-page vs absolute is decided by `decodeBits`, which returns 16-bit for every label
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] unconditionally — forward-referenced zero-page labels always assemble as absolute. Pass 2 could
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] re-pick the mode once the value is known.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - Labels are only recorded `if(currentAddress > 0)`, so anything at $0000 or before the first
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] `.org` is silently dropped.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - Byte counts come from hex-string length (`((hexBytes.length+1)/3)|0`), which papers over
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] malformed output instead of catching it — counting actual emitted bytes would double as a sanity
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] check.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - `hex8` has a path with no return; the `pad` computation in `assemble6502` will throw on labels
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] longer than 23 chars; semicolons inside quoted strings will be mistaken for comments.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - The auto-create-on-miss behaviour in `findLabel` means a typo'd operand assembles cleanly to a
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] phantom symbol in both passes — arguably pass 2 should report unknowns.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] - Housekeeping: unused `closeDevices` import, and the commented-out `logLabels()`/`logEquates()`
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] calls suggest you were mid-debug on exactly the paths above.
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] If it's any consolation, the two-pass skeleton, the equate/label/comment separation, and the
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] JSON-driven instruction table are a reasonable foundation — the failures are concentrated in the
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] operand-decode layer, which is also the part that's most testable in isolation. A handful of tiny
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] fixtures (one line per addressing mode per spec shape, compared against known-good bytes) would pin
2026-08-07 18:19:32 [kimi-k3] down every issue above.
2026-08-07 18:19:33 [kimi-k3] 
