2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] Yes — `<p>` in HTML5 has what's called an "optional end tag." You don't need `</p>` at all; the
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] parser automatically closes it when it hits certain other block-level elements (another `<p>`, a
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] heading, a `<div>`, a `<ul>`, etc.). That's exactly what's happening in this file already — the
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] `<p>` before "raw payload..." has no closing tag and it's perfectly valid HTML.
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] This is different from XHTML, where every element must be explicitly closed (`<p>...</p>`
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] or self-closed). Since this doc has no XML declaration or `xhtml` doctype, you're free to leave
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] `<p>` unclosed as you're already doing.
2026-08-10 06:55:58 [claude-sonnet-5] 
