2026-08-10 07:04:12 [nitro@ryzen5] chrome says translated to english when reading so of my json files?
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.018 43.66KB 14.94s
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] That happens because Chrome runs its language-detection heuristic on any text content it renders in
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] the tab — including raw JSON viewed directly in the browser — and decides whether to offer
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] translation based on what language it thinks the bulk of the visible text is in.
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] Given what's in these files — Cherokee syllabary, Coptic, Greek, Hebrew, Vai, Ogham, hieroglyph
2026-08-10 07:04:27 [claude-sonnet-5] codepoints, fullwidth Latin — it's not surprising Chrome's detector gets confused. If a JSON file
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] has a lot of non-Latin script values packed together, the detector can decide the "page language"
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] is something other than English and pop the translate banner, even though structurally it's just
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] JSON key/value data, not prose in another language.
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] A few things that influence this:
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] - No `lang` attribute or `Content-Language` header — when serving JSON directly (not
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] wrapped in HTML), there's nothing telling Chrome "this is English content with embedded non-English
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] strings." It falls back to sniffing the raw text.
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] - Ratio of script matters — a file like `machinespec.json` (mostly English prose in values)
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] is less likely to trigger it than something like the Cherokee or Vai syllabary lists, where huge
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] blocks of non-Latin characters dominate the sample Chrome scans.
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] - This is purely a browser-side UX thing — it doesn't affect the actual data or how
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] `bibli.js` parses/uses the JSON. It's Chrome being "helpful" when you view the raw file directly in
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] a tab.
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] If it bothers you and you want to suppress it specifically for the raw JSON views, the usual fix is
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] either serving the file with an explicit `Content-Language: en` header, or (if wrapping it in an
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] HTML view instead of raw JSON) adding `<html lang="en">`. Since your actual data display goes
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] through `index.html` and `bibli.js` rather than users opening the raw `.json` directly, this is
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] likely only something you notice yourself when spot-checking files in the browser — not something
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] end users viewing the rendered page would hit.
2026-08-10 07:04:28 [claude-sonnet-5] 
