2026-08-10 07:05:25 [nitro@ryzen5]  U+1300x	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??# U+1301x	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??	??
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.018 46.05KB 10.88s
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] That's the same root cause, just showing up on the render side instead of the detection side.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] When Chrome (or GitHub's markdown renderer, or VS Code's preview) displays that markdown file, it's
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] rendering actual Unicode codepoints for a given block — presumably Ethiopic or similar given the
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] `U+1300x` / `U+1301x` range labels — but wherever you're viewing it doesn't have a font installed
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] that covers those glyphs. So instead of the real character, you get the tofu/fallback `??` boxes.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] This is different from the translation-banner issue: that was Chrome's language detector
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] getting confused by the text content. This is a font coverage problem — the renderer knows
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] what codepoint to draw, it just has no glyph for it in any font available on that system, so it
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] substitutes a placeholder.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] A few things worth knowing:
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] - `U+1300`–`U+137F` is the Ethiopic block — if that's what you're cataloguing, you'd need a
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] font like Noto Sans Ethiopic, Abyssinica SIL, or similar installed system-wide (or referenced via
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] `@font-face` if this is going through your `bibli.css`) for the glyphs to actually render.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] - This is viewer-dependent, not data-dependent — the underlying codepoints in the `.md`
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] file are almost certainly correct; it's purely about whether the machine reading the file has the
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] right font. Same file could look perfect on one machine and be all `??` boxes on another.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] - GitHub's own markdown renderer typically has broad Unicode font fallback baked in via web
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] fonts, so if this is `hieroglyph.md` or a similar block-listing file destined for GitHub Pages, it
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] might render fine there even if it looks broken locally in whatever editor/preview you're using.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] Is this happening in a local markdown preview (VS Code, etc.) or when viewing it rendered on GitHub
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] itself? That'll tell you whether it's a "your machine needs a font" issue or something to actually
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] fix in the pipeline.
2026-08-10 07:05:36 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:03 [nitro@ryzen5] any standard for labelling markdown content with lang?
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.027 48.21KB 19.24s
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] There's no native CommonMark/GFM syntax for this — markdown itself has no `lang` attribute
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] mechanism. But a few conventions exist depending on what you're trying to solve:
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • 1. YAML frontmatter (whole-document language)*
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] Common in Jekyll/Hugo/most static site generators:
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] ---
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] lang: en
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] ---
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] This tags the document as a whole, which doesn't really help for a file that's mostly
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] Unicode codepoint tables rather than prose in one language.
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • 2. Inline HTML passthrough (per-block/per-span)*
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] Since markdown allows raw HTML to pass through untouched, you can wrap specific content:
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] <span lang="am">ሀለሐመሠረሰሸቀ</span>
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] or for a whole section:
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] <div lang="am">
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] # U+1300x	ሀ	ለ	ሐ	መ	ሠ	ረ	ሰ	ሸ
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] </div>
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] This is honestly the most correct fix for your Ethiopic block case, since `lang` on a wrapping
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] element is exactly what screen readers and browser rendering/font-fallback logic look at.
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • 3. Pandoc-style span/div attributes*
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] If you're using Pandoc as your markdown processor, there's an extended syntax:
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] [ሀለሐመ]{lang="am"}
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] but this is Pandoc-specific, not portable to GitHub's renderer or most other parsers.
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] • Worth noting for your case specifically: a codepoint chart isn't really "written in"
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] a language — it's data, not prose. Tagging `lang="am"` on an Ethiopic glyph table is a bit of a
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] category mismatch (there's no Amharic sentence being spoken). The `lang` attribute's real
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] value here is more pragmatic than semantic: it hints to the renderer "use a font/fallback chain
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] that can render this script,"* which is exactly the tofu-box problem you ran into. So even if it's
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] not linguistically perfect, wrapping unicode block tables in `<div lang="am">` (or whatever's
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] closest) is likely to actually fix the rendering rather than just being decorative.
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] If GitHub Pages is the target renderer, worth testing whether it respects `lang` on inline
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] `<div>`/`<span>` for font fallback — GitHub's own CSS does pull in fairly broad web font coverage
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] already, so the `??` boxes might be a local-editor-only problem rather than something that needs
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] fixing in the markdown itself.
2026-08-10 07:06:23 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:07:52 [nitro@ryzen5] other markdown conventions, i am interested in lore
2026-08-10 07:08:12 [claude-sonnet-5] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 claude-sonnet-5 A\ 🪠 🧊 $0.027 50.60KB 19.21s
2026-08-10 07:08:12 [claude-sonnet-5] "Lore" in the markdown-convention sense is a fun rabbit hole — there's a fair bit of
2026-08-10 07:08:12 [claude-sonnet-5] folklore/tribal-knowledge around markdown that never made it into any spec but persists because
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] tools or communities adopted it. A few threads worth knowing about:
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • The "flavors" schism*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Markdown was never really one thing after John Gruber's original 2004 spec — it was famously
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] underspecified, which led to a decade of incompatible implementations (PHP Markdown Extra,
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Maruku, Redcarpet, Discount...) all disagreeing on edge cases like nested lists or emphasis inside
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] words. This is the actual reason CommonMark exists — an attempt in 2014 to write a formal,
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] unambiguous grammar so two parsers would agree on the same input. It's telling that even now,
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] "markdown" without qualification is meaningless without knowing whose markdown.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • GFM's quiet feature creep*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] GitHub Flavored Markdown added a bunch of things over the years that leaked into general assumption
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] despite never being part of core markdown: task lists (`- [ ] todo`), tables via pipes,
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] `~~strikethrough~~`, automatic URL linking, and `@mentions`/`#issue` autolinking (GitHub-specific,
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] invisible elsewhere). A lot of people write "markdown" today that's really unrenderable outside
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] GitHub's own pipeline.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • The "setext" headers nobody uses*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Original markdown supports underlining text with `===` or `---` to make it an H1/H2:
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Title
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] =====
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Almost nobody does this anymore in favor of `# Title`, but it's still valid everywhere and
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] occasionally you'll see it in older READMEs like a fossil.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • Reference-style links — the forgotten feature*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] [text][1]
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] [1]: https://example.com
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Designed for cleaner plain-text readability (so the URL doesn't clutter the prose), barely used now
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] that most editors auto-format inline links, but it's a genuinely nice convention for documents with
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] heavy citation/link density — which, actually, might suit a project like yours with lots of
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] external references.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • The lone backslash escape mess*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Every implementation has slightly different rules about which punctuation needs escaping (`\*`,
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] `\_`, `\[`) and it's one of the most common cross-renderer breakage points — something that looks
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] fine on GitHub can render wrong in VS Code preview or Obsidian.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • Emoji shortcodes as folklore, not spec*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] `:tada:` → 🎉 is pure GitHub/Slack convention, not markdown at all — it's a separate
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] text-replacement pass bolted on by specific platforms. If you're already doing raw Unicode emoji
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] directly in your files, you're bypassing this shortcode-lore entirely, which is arguably the
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] "purer" way of doing it.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] • Admonition blocks — the multi-tool disagreement*
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Things like:
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] > **Note:** some text
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] vs. Obsidian/Docusaurus-style:
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] > [!NOTE]
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] > some text
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] are a great example of convention without standard — everyone invented their own "callout
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] box" syntax and none of them agree, so what renders as a fancy colored box in Obsidian is just a
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] plain blockquote with literal `[!NOTE]` text on GitHub.
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] Given your project's flavor — lots of raw Unicode, multilingual scripts, bibliographic/spec
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] cataloguing — the CommonMark vs GFM tension and the reference-link convention are probably the two
2026-08-10 07:08:13 [claude-sonnet-5] most practically relevant bits of lore for you. Is there a particular corner of this you
2026-08-10 07:08:14 [claude-sonnet-5] want to go deeper on — like whether GitHub Pages' renderer (Jekyll + kramdown, typically) has its
2026-08-10 07:08:14 [claude-sonnet-5] own quirks worth knowing?
2026-08-10 07:08:14 [claude-sonnet-5] 
