2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] Given the sheer script diversity here — Cherokee, Vai, Ogham, Coptic, Linear B, Ethiopic,
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] hieroglyphs, alchemical symbols, box-drawing, emoji — no single font covers it all. Even large
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] "universal" fonts have gaps in the more obscure historic blocks. So the practical answer is a
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] stack, not one font.
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] • For actual glyph coverage (the "no tofu" fallback layer):*
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] The Noto family (Google) exists specifically to solve this problem — "Noto" literally
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] stands for "no tofu." Most of your rarer blocks have dedicated Noto fonts:
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Cherokee
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Vai
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Ogham
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Coptic
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Ethiopic
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Linear B
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Egyptian Hieroglyphs
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] - Noto Sans Symbols / Symbols2 (covers alchemical, math symbols, box-drawing, most of your
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] `fenced.code` geometry set)
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] You'd reference these as a layered `font-family` fallback chain in `bibli.css`, letting the browser
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] pick whichever installed/loaded font actually has the glyph.
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] • For project "look" / identity (headers, prose):*
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] Given the "grimoire" framing in the README, something with old-book/mystical character but still
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] clean would suit the branding — EB Garamond, Cormorant, or IM Fell English
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] all read as antique/manuscript-like without being illegible. Any of these as the primary display
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] font, falling back to Noto Sans for anything the display font can't render, gives you both
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] character and coverage.
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] • For your `<pre>` blocks (code/data/status/terminal):*
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] Since those already read like a status/terminal aesthetic, a monospace with decent symbol coverage
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] — JetBrains Mono or Cascadia Code — would fit and both handle box-drawing
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] characters and many symbol blocks reasonably well natively.
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] • Practical CSS shape:*
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] font-family: "EB Garamond", "Noto Sans", "Noto Sans Symbols2", sans-serif;
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] for prose/headers, and a separate monospace stack for the `<pre>` elements.
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] Want me to draft the actual `@font-face`/`font-family` rules for `bibli.css` once you pick a
2026-08-10 07:13:50 [claude-sonnet-5] direction, or are you just gathering options for now?
