2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] Fair pushback — let me clarify what's actually happening, because there's a misunderstanding here
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] rather than laziness on either side.
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] CSS custom properties don't have any concept of "inherit the variable name from my own id."
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] `var(--code-bg)` is just a static reference — the string `code-bg` isn't computed from the
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] element's `id`, it's literally typed by you (or me) in the stylesheet. CSS has no templating or
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] string-interpolation mechanism to do something like `var(--#{id}-bg)`. That's not a CSS limitation
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] I'm working around badly — it genuinely doesn't exist in the language.
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] So there are really only two honest options:
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] • Option A — one shared variable, same background everywhere:*
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] :root{
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 	--pre-bg: #f6f6f6;
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] }
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] pre{
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 	background-color: var(--pre-bg);
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] }
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] Every `<pre>` gets the same background from one source of truth. Simple, but not "per-id."
2026-08-10 07:20:43 [claude-sonnet-5] 
