2026-08-10 10:25:43 [nitro@ryzen5] the key stream encoding in terminals is an ansi standard?
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 kimi-k3 🜁 🪠 🧊 $0.032 513B 57.33s
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] Partly. The framing is standardized, but the actual key encodings are mostly de facto conventions.
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] • What is standardized:* The structure of the byte sequences comes from ECMA-48 (ISO/IEC
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 6429), which is essentially identical to the withdrawn ANSI X3.64-1979 — that's where the term
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] "ANSI escape codes" comes from. It defines the grammar: the ESC introducer, CSI (`ESC [`), SS3
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] (`ESC O`), parameter bytes, and final bytes. Separately, the plain control characters (Ctrl+A =
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 0x01, Tab = 0x09, Esc = 0x1B) come from ASCII/ISO 646, which is why Tab and Ctrl+I are
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] indistinguishable, as are Enter and Ctrl+M.
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] • What isn't: ECMA-48 mainly covers control functions sent to the terminal.
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] What a terminal sends when you press* a key was defined by whoever made the device, and DEC's
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] choices became the baseline everyone copies:
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] - Arrow keys → `CSI A/B/C/D` (or `SS3` in application mode) — VT100
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] - Home/End/Insert/Delete/PgUp/PgDn → `CSI H/F/2~/3~/5~/6~` — VT220-ish
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] - F5+ → `CSI 1x~` — VT220
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] - Modifier parameters like `CSI 1;5A` for Ctrl+Up — xterm's invention, widely imitated, not in any
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] formal standard
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] There's real divergence too: Backspace sending BS vs DEL, rxvt's different Home/End encodings,
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] application-mode variants. This is exactly why terminfo/termcap exists — a database to paper over
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] the fact that there's no single agreed encoding.
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] Modern terminals are still patching the gaps. The kitty keyboard protocol and the fixterms proposal
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] exist specifically because the legacy encoding is ambiguous (you can't tell ESC alone from Alt+key,
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] Ctrl+arrows needed bolt-on extensions, etc.).
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] So: "ANSI" is the colloquial name, ECMA-48 is the actual standard for the sequence format, and the
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] key mappings themselves are DEC/xterm folklore.
2026-08-10 10:26:41 [kimi-k3] 
