2026-08-13 07:59:52 [nitro@ryzen5] does spec profile make sense for the 6502 json?
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.6-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.154 242.1KB 9.79s
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] Yes, `specprofile` makes structural sense in your schema—it acts as a lookup table where
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] each instruction’s `"spec"` field serves as an index into `specprofile`. This allows you to decode
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] the byte lengths and cycle counts for each addressing mode corresponding to the space-separated
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] list of opcodes in `"opcodes"`.
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] How `specprofile` Maps to Instructions
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] For an instruction with N opcodes in its `"opcodes"` string, the profile at index `spec` provides
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] parallel arrays of N byte lengths and N cycle counts.
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] Example 1: Single Mode Instructions
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `NOP` (`spec: 0`, opcode `"EA"`)
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   `specprofile[0]` \rightarrow `bytes: [1]`, `cycles: [2]`
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   1 byte, 2 cycles.
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `JSR` (`spec: 6`, opcode `"20"`)
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   `specprofile[6]` \rightarrow `bytes: [3]`, `cycles: [6]`
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   3 bytes, 6 cycles.
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] Example 2: Multi-Mode Arithmetic (`LDA`, `ADC`, `AND`, `EOR`, `ORA`, `SBC`, `CMP`)
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] • `LDA` (`spec: 15`, opcodes `"A9 A5 B5 AD BD B9 A1 B1"`)
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   `specprofile[15]` \rightarrow
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   * `bytes`: `[2, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 2, 2]`
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]   * `cycles`: `[2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 6, 5]`
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] Matching opcode positions to addressing modes and `specprofile[15]`:
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] ╭────────────┬──────────┬───────────────────┬─────────┬──────────╮
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  Position  │  Opcode  │  Addressing Mode  │  Bytes  │  Cycles  │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] ├────────────┼──────────┼───────────────────┼─────────┼──────────┤
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  0         │  `A9`    │  Immediate        │  2      │  2       │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  1         │  `A5`    │  Zero Page        │  2      │  3       │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  2         │  `B5`    │  Zero Page,X      │  2      │  4       │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  3         │  `AD`    │  Absolute         │  3      │  4       │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  4         │  `BD`    │  Absolute,X       │  3      │  4*      │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  5         │  `B9`    │  Absolute,Y       │  3      │  4*      │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  6         │  `A1`    │  (Indirect,X)     │  2      │  6       │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] │  7         │  `B1`    │  (Indirect),Y     │  2      │  5*      │
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] ╰────────────┴──────────┴───────────────────┴─────────┴──────────╯
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] • \ Note: Base cycles shown; page-boundary crossing adds 1 cycle in hardware.
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] Key Considerations for 6502 Fidelity
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] While the indexing scheme is clean and compact, keep the following 6502 edge cases in mind when
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] using `specprofile`:
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash] 1. Page-Boundary Penalties (+1 Cycle)
2026-08-13 08:00:04 [gemini-3.6-flash]    Modes like `Absolute,X`, `Absolute,Y`, and `(Indirect),Y` incur an additional cycle when a page
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] boundary is crossed during read operations. Since this is dynamic, storing base cycles in
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] `specprofile` and adding penalty logic in the CPU tick loop is standard practice.
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] 2. Branch Timing (`BCC`, `BCS`, `BEQ`, etc.)
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    `specprofile[5]` lists `bytes: [2]`, `cycles: [2]`.
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * 2 cycles if the branch is not taken.
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * 3 cycles if taken on the same page.
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * 4 cycles if taken across a page boundary.
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] 3. `INC` / `DEC` Memory vs. Register
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * Memory `INC`/`DEC` uses `spec: 11` (`[2, 2, 3, 3]` bytes \rightarrow `[5, 6, 6, 7]` cycles).
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    * Register `INX`/`DEX`/`INY`/`DEY` uses `spec: 0` (`[1]` byte \rightarrow `[2]` cycles).
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash]    Keeping these separate in `specprofile` correctly accounts for read-modify-write cycle overhead
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] on memory.
2026-08-13 08:00:05 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:00:44 [nitro@ryzen5] for many spec profiles will be needed for 6800?
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.6-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.184 245.6KB 21.85s
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] To fully define the Motorola 6800 instruction set using this schema, you need 16 spec
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] profiles (indexed `0` through `15`).
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] Because Motorola organized the 6800 opcodes into regular addressing mode blocks, its timing
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] patterns condense into 16 profile entries (matching the count of the 6502).
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:01:07 [gemini-3.6-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
