2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] Addressing Modes Used by Z80 Branch Instructions
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] The Z80's "branch" instructions — jumps, calls, returns, and restart — use four of the ten
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] addressing modes, depending on the specific opcode:
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Relative (used by `JR` and `DJNZ`)
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The operand is an 8-bit signed displacement added to the PC after the instruction.
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Only for short jumps within −128 to +127 bytes.
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] JR  label
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] JR  NZ, label
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] DJNZ loop       ; decrement B, jump if non-zero
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Extended / Immediate Extended (used by `JP` and `CALL`)
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] - A full 16-bit absolute address follows the opcode.
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] - This is the "direct" addressing mode for long jumps and subroutine calls.
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] JP  nnnn        ; unconditional absolute jump
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] JP  C, nnnn     ; conditional absolute jump
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] CALL nnnn       ; call subroutine at address nnnn
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] CALL NZ, nnnn   ; conditional call
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:50 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Register Indirect (used by `JP (HL)`, `JP (IX)`, `JP (IY)`)
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The 16-bit address is taken from a register pair or index register.
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Note: the parentheses indicate indirection: jump to the address stored in
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] HL/IX/IY.
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] JP  (HL)
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] JP  (IX)
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] JP  (IY)
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. Modified Page Zero (used by `RST`)
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - A special call to a fixed address in the first 256 bytes (00h, 08h, … 38h).
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The opcode itself encodes the destination, so no explicit operand is needed.
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] RST 10h         ; call address 0010h
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 5. Implied (used by `RET`, `RETI`, `RETN`)
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] - No explicit operand; the return address is taken from the stack implicitly (SP).
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] RET             ; return from subroutine
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] RET NZ          ; conditional return
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] RETI            ; return from interrupt
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] RETN            ; return from non-maskable interrupt
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Summary table:*
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╭─────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────╮
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Instruction group  │  Addressing mode(s)             │
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] ├─────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────┤
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  `JR`, `DJNZ`       │  Relative                       │
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  `JP nn`, `CALL nn` │  Extended (immediate extended)  │
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  `JP (HL/IX/IY)`    │  Register indirect              │
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  `RST`              │  Modified page zero             │
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  `RET` family       │  Implied (stack)                │
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╰─────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────╯
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] So if by "branched" you mean branching/control-flow instructions, they collectively use
2026-08-13 08:38:51 [deepseek-v4-flash] relative, extended, register indirect, modified page zero, and implied addressing modes.
