2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] From the JSON you supplied:
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Instruction mnemonics (specs): 67
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Addressing modes: 10
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] That list of mnemonics includes many that expand to multiple opcodes; for example, `LD`, `JP`,
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] `CALL`, and `ADD` each cover many operand/addressing-mode combinations. The JSON itself does not
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] contain per-mnemonic opcode counts, so the actual “unique opcode” tally cannot be derived from it
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] alone.
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you need Z80 opcode-count figures, those come from the full opcode maps (primary, CB, ED, DD, FD
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] pages). Typical quoted figures are:
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - 158 documented instruction types (mnemonics)
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - 252 documented opcode variants when all addressing modes are counted
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - 10 addressing modes (as Zilog defines them)
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] But that depends on how you count undocumented opcodes and prefix combinations.
2026-08-13 08:40:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:41:49 [nitro@ryzen5] how about we use a 10 bit mask to identify which addressing modes an instruction supports?
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 8.910KB 12.02s
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes, a 10‑bit mask is an excellent way to compactly encode which addressing modes an instruction
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] can use. It makes programmatic checks trivial — for example, `(mask & (1 << BIT_INDEXED)) != 0`
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] tells you whether an indexed variant exists.
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] Here's how you could map your existing `addressmode` array to fixed bit positions:
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 0: immediate
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 1: immediate extended
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 2: modified page zero
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 3: relative
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 4: extended
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 5: indexed
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 6: register
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 7: register indirect
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 8: implied
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] bit 9: bit address
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] Then each instruction object in your JSON could include a field like `"addrModesMask"`:
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] "LD": {
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "addrModesMask": 0b01101111
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] Or, for readability, you could list the modes as an array and compute the mask at load time:
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] "LD": {
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "addressModes": [
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "immediate",
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "immediate extended",
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "extended",
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "indexed",
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "register",
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "register indirect"
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash]   ]
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] Both work; the mask is just a more compact serialisation.
2026-08-13 08:42:02 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] • Caveats to keep in mind:*
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] - The mask describes all addressing modes used by any variant of the instruction. A single
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] variant like `LD (HL), n` actually uses two modes at once (register indirect and immediate), but
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] the mask still captures both.
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] - If you later decide to document individual opcodes or variants, you'll need a separate mask per
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] variant. The mnemonic‑level mask is a convenient summary, not a full opcode map.
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Because there are exactly 10 modes, a 10‑bit mask fits neatly into a `uint16`, leaving six unused
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] bits for future extensions.
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] This approach is clean, human‑readable, and machine‑efficient. I'd say go for it.
2026-08-13 08:42:03 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
