2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] Example JSON:
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] "ADC": {
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "modes": "1000011100",
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "label": "add with carry",
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "flags": "SZHPNC"
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] Just be aware that this reads backwards compared to normal binary notation. To avoid
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] misinterpretation later, either:
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Document the convention clearly (e.g., in your spec: “`modes` is a 10‑char string, LSB first”),
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] or
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Use an array of mode names instead:
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] "ADC": {
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "modes": ["immediate", "indexed", "register", "register indirect"],
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash]   ...
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] Both are human‑readable; the array is more verbose but self‑documenting.
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] Also note that `ADC` affects all six flags, so you may want to fill in `"flags": "SZHPNC"`.
2026-08-13 08:45:59 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:46:58 [nitro@ryzen5] so it has 4 opcodes or more due to mode bits?
2026-08-13 08:47:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 12.96KB 9.67s
2026-08-13 08:47:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] The 10‑bit mode mask identifies which addressing modes a mnemonic uses, but not how
2026-08-13 08:47:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] many opcodes it has. For `ADC`, the four modes expand to 15 distinct opcodes/encodings:
2026-08-13 08:47:08 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╭─────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────╮
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Addressing mode    │  Variants                                                   │  Opcode count  │
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] ├─────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────┤
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Register           │  ADC A, A/B/C/D/E/H/L                                       │  7             │
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Register indirect  │  ADC A, (HL)                                                │  1             │
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Immediate          │  ADC A, n                                                   │  1             │
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Indexed            │  ADC A, (IX+d) / (IY+d)                                     │  2             │
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  —                  │  ADC HL, BC/DE/HL/SP (uses register mode, but 16‑bit form)  │  4             │
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╰─────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────╯
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] Total 15.
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] So the mode mask says “ADC can use these four addressing modes”, but the actual opcode
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] count is larger because the same mode can combine with different register choices, 8‑ vs 16‑bit
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] operands, and index prefixes.
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want to store opcode counts in your spec, a separate field like `"opcodeCount": 15` or a
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] detailed variant list would be needed. The mask alone is only a high‑level summary.
2026-08-13 08:47:09 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:48:14 [nitro@ryzen5] opcode without register encoding, are they always dequential in opcode assign?
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 14.78KB 21.62s
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] No — not always. In the Z80, opcodes for instructions that don't encode a register are not
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] assigned in a single, strictly sequential list. The opcode map is organized by functional groups,
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] and individual opcodes may be:
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Sequentially placed within a block – e.g., RST: 0x00, 0x08, 0x10, … 0x38 (step 8).
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Scattered across unrelated instructions – e.g., NOP = 0x00, HALT = 0x76, DI = 0xF3, EI =
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] 0xFB.
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Grouped by condition bits – e.g., conditional calls: 0xC4, 0xCC, 0xD4, 0xDC, … (the
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] condition field orders them in a predictable pattern, but they're not a single run).
2026-08-13 08:48:36 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Placed under a prefix – e.g., ADC HL,BC = 0xED 0x4A, ADC HL,DE = 0xED 0x5A, ADC HL,HL =
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] 0xED 0x6A, ADC HL,SP = 0xED 0x7A. Those are sequential in the high nibble of the second
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] byte, but only within the ED-prefixed block.
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] So if you're building a spec, you can't derive the actual opcode value from the mnemonic + mode
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] mask alone. The mask tells you which addressing modes exist, but the exact opcode (and even
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] the number of opcodes) depends on the Z80's internal encoding scheme — which mixes register fields,
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] condition fields, and fixed bit patterns.
2026-08-13 08:48:37 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:01 [nitro@ryzen5] the question is for those that encode a register, and those that encode a register pair
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 16.21KB 21.30s
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Let me answer both parts:
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Instructions with register encoding
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Yes — they follow a fixed, regular pattern in the opcode map. The register (or register
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] pair) is encoded directly into specific bits of the opcode, so the resulting opcodes are not
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] sequential as `+1` steps but are arithmetic progressions with a constant step.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Single registers (8‑bit)
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] The register code is a 3‑bit field, usually `r = 000,001,010,011,100,101,110,111` for
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] `B,C,D,E,H,L,(HL),A`.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Example: `LD r, n` has format `00 r 110`. The opcodes are:
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD B,n  0x06   (r=000)
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD C,n  0x0E   (r=001)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD D,n  0x16   (r=010)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD E,n  0x1E   (r=011)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD H,n  0x26   (r=100)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD L,n  0x2E   (r=101)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD (HL),n 0x36 (r=110)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD A,n  0x3E   (r=111)   +8
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] So they are sequential in steps of 8.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] The exception is the `(HL)` entry, but it still fits the pattern.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Similarly, `LD r, r'` (`0x40 + r*8 + r'`) gives 64 opcodes in a single contiguous block
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] `0x40–0x7F`, ordered by the `r` and `r'` fields.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Register pairs (16‑bit)
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] Most pair‑based instructions use a 2‑bit field `rp`:
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] - For `LD rr,nn`, `ADD HL,rr`, `ADC HL,rr`, etc., the mapping is:
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash]   `00 = BC`, `01 = DE`, `10 = HL`, `11 = SP`.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash]   Format: `00 rp0 001`, so opcodes are:
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD BC,nn  0x01
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD DE,nn  0x11   +0x10
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD HL,nn  0x21   +0x10
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] LD SP,nn  0x31   +0x10
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] A clean step of `0x10`.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] - For `PUSH`/`POP`, the mapping is `00 = BC`, `01 = DE`, `10 = HL`, `11 = AF`.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash]   Format: `11 rp0 101`, giving `0xC5, 0xD5, 0xE5, 0xF5`.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] - Conditional jumps/calls/returns use a 3‑bit condition field that also follows a regular order
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] (`000 = NZ, 001 = Z, 010 = NC, 011 = C, 100 = PO, 101 = PE, 110 = P, 111 = M`), yielding opcodes
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] spaced by 8.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] • So for any instruction family that carries a register or condition field, the opcodes are
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] assigned in a predictable, increasing order according to that field.* That’s why assemblers can
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] often compute the opcode from the operands.
2026-08-13 08:49:23 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] Instructions without register encoding
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] Here the opcodes are not systematic — they are chosen individually, often with no
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] arithmetic relationship. Examples:
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] NOP       0x00
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] HALT      0x76
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] DI        0xF3
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] EI        0xFB
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] CPL       0x2F
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] SCF       0x37
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] CCF       0x3F
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] DAA       0x27
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] RLA       0x17
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] RRCA      0x0F
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] These do not occupy a sequential block. Even within a related group, e.g. rotate instructions:
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] RLCA  0x07
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] RLA   0x17
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] RRCA  0x0F
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] RRA   0x1F
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] They are scattered, though they share a bit‑pattern (`00 xxx 111` with a 3‑bit "operation" field).
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] But the point is: you cannot infer the opcode from a simple sequence.
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] Summary
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╭─────────────────────────┬────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Encoding               │  Opcode assignment                                         │
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] ├─────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Register (3‑bit)       │  Always a fixed step (typically +8) per register code      │
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Register pair (2‑bit)  │  Always a fixed step (+0x10) per pair code                 │
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Condition (3‑bit)      │  Always a fixed step (+8) per condition code               │
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  No register/condition  │  Arbitrary, no sequence; based on functional bit grouping  │
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╰─────────────────────────┴────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] So if you are building a spec or a disassembler: for instructions that encode a register or
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] condition, you can rely on the arithmetic progression. For those that don’t, you need explicit
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] opcode tables.
2026-08-13 08:49:24 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:50:51 [nitro@ryzen5] so if opcodes do not populate these two fields we can do a opocode count same as mode count for adc?
2026-08-13 08:50:59 [nitro@ryzen5] 		"ADC":{"modes":"1000011100","opcodes":[],"label":"add with carry","flags":"SZHPNC"},
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 20.23KB 11.83s
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] The short answer is no — for `ADC` the opcode count is not equal to the mode count.
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] The mask tells you which addressing modes exist, but not how many opcodes exist
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] within each mode.
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] Why
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╭─────────────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┬────────────────╮
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Mode               │  ADC variants                                       │  Opcode count  │
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] ├─────────────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┼────────────────┤
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Immediate          │  `ADC A, n`                                         │  1             │
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Register           │  `ADC A, r` (7 choices) + `ADC HL, rr` (4 choices)  │  11            │
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Register indirect  │  `ADC A, (HL)`                                      │  1             │
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] │  Indexed            │  `ADC A, (IX+d)` + `ADC A, (IY+d)`                  │  2             │
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] ╰─────────────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────────────────┴────────────────╯
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] Total: 15 opcodes, while the mode mask has only 4 bits set.
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] So if you set `"opcodes":[]` and expect it to represent "one per mode", that will be wrong for
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] instructions that select registers/register pairs within a mode.
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] When would the count match?
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] Only for instructions that have exactly one fixed encoding per mode — e.g. `NOP` (implied
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] only, 1 opcode) or `CCF` (implied only, 1 opcode). But most Z80 instructions with register operands
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] expand.
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] Better representation
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want to keep it human‑readable, either:
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Store an explicit `"opcodeCount"`:
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] "ADC": {
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "modes": "1000011100",
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "opcodeCount": 15,
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "label": "add with carry",
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "flags": "SZHPNC"
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:51:11 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Store the actual variant list (more verbose but complete):
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] "ADC": {
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "modes": ["immediate", "register", "register indirect", "indexed"],
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "variants": [
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "ADC A,n",
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "ADC A,BC", "ADC A,DE", "ADC A,HL", "ADC A,SP",
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "ADC A,B", "ADC A,C", "ADC A,D", "ADC A,E", "ADC A,H", "ADC A,L", "ADC A,A",
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "ADC A,(HL)",
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]     "ADC A,(IX+d)", "ADC A,(IY+d)"
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]   ],
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "label": "add with carry",
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "flags": "SZHPNC"
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] The empty `"opcodes":[]` array is a bit ambiguous — it could mean "no opcodes listed" rather than
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] "opcode count equals mode count". If you want to use it as a count, rename it to make that
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] explicit.
2026-08-13 08:51:12 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:51:57 [nitro@ryzen5] let me rename it to help with my thinking
