2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 25.43KB 21.99s
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] Understood — if you're happy to lose the extra detail, that's your call. The simplified `opbits`
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] array can have one entry per set bit in `modes`, but you have to decide what to store for modes
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] that have multiple encodings.
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] For example, `ADC` has 4 modes in your mask, so `opbits` could look like:
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash] "ADC": {
2026-08-13 08:54:54 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "modes": "1000011100",
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "opbits": ["CE", "88", "8E", "DD8E"],
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "label": "add with carry",
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash]   "flags": "SZHPNC"
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] }
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] Mapping:
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `CE` → immediate
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `88` → register (masked, for `ADC A,r` with r=000)
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `8E` → register indirect
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `DD8E` → indexed (using IX; IY version is omitted for simplicity)
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] But note: `register` also has the `ADC HL,rr` forms (`ED 4A`, etc.), and `indexed` has both IX and
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] IY variants. Those are lost in this version. That's fine if your spec doesn't need them.
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] If you want to keep the array exactly one byte per mode, you could store only the primary opcode
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] byte and ignore prefixes:
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] "opbits": ["CE", "88", "8E", "8E"]
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] …but then the prefixes (`DD`) and the duplicate `8E` make it ambiguous. Using strings like `"DD8E"`
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] is clearer while still being compact.
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] Either way, the key is that `opbits` is now a list of representative masked opcodes, one per mode,
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] not a claim about total opcode count. That's a reasonable trade-off for a human-readable summary.
2026-08-13 08:54:55 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
