2026-08-15 06:25:49 [nitro@ryzen5] addressing modes of 6800 cpu
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.003 494B 25.14s
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] The Motorola 6800 CPU has 6 addressing modes:
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 1. Inherent (Implied)
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    No operand is needed; the instruction implies what to do.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Examples: `NOP`, `INX`, `DEX`, `CLRA`, `TAB`
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 2. Immediate
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The operand is a constant value in the instruction.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Indicated with `#`.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Example: `LDAA #$20`
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 3. Direct
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    An 8-bit address is given; the high byte of the address is assumed to be `$00` (zero page).
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Example: `LDAA 20` means load from address `0020`
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 4. Extended
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    A full 16-bit address is given.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Example: `LDAA $2000`
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 5. Indexed
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The effective address is formed by adding an 8-bit offset to the X register.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    The 6800 only has the X index register.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Example: `LDAA 10,X` loads from address `X + 10`
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 6. Relative
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Used for branch instructions.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    An 8-bit signed offset is added to the program counter.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash]    Example: `BRA label`, `BNE label`
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] Some sources also mention an Accumulator mode for instructions like `ASLA`, `COMA`, etc.,
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] but those are usually classified as inherent.
2026-08-15 06:26:16 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:56:07 [nitro@ryzen5] in 6502 is this a legal opcode?
2026-08-15 06:56:07 [nitro@ryzen5] unsupported opcode 110 ($6E)
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.001 1.837KB 6.85s
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] In the 6502, opcode `$6E` (hex) is legal.
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] It is the official instruction:
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `ROR absolute` — Rotate Right memory location (16-bit address).
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] Example: `ROR $1234`
2026-08-15 06:56:14 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] So if an emulator or disassembler reports `$6E` as “unsupported,” that is incorrect for a standard
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 6502.
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] Just to avoid confusion:
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - `$6E` = 110 decimal
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - On the 6800, `$6E` is also legal — it is `JMP indexed`.
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] - But on the 6502, `$6E` is ROR absolute.
2026-08-15 06:56:15 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
