2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.002 2.534KB 17.52s
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] The HALT instruction is the end-of-display-list marker for the Atari DVG (Digital
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Vector Generator).
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] What it does
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] When the DVG encounters the HALT opcode while reading the vector list from memory, it stops all
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] further processing:
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - It stops moving the electron beam.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - It sets a status/flag (e.g., the “HALT” condition) that the CPU can detect.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] - It does not blank the beam or alter the current position; it simply ceases operation.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] The CPU can check this flag to know that the current frame’s vector list has been fully executed.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] After updating the vector list for the next frame, the CPU starts the DVG again by writing to the
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] “GO” (start) register.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Instruction encoding
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] On the Atari DVG (used in games like Asteroids and Lunar Lander), the HALT
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] instruction is typically the 16-bit word `0x0000` (all bits zero). In the 4-bit opcode
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] field, this corresponds to opcode `$0`.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] So a typical display list ends with:
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] ...   (drawing commands)
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] HALT  ; 0x0000
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Why it matters
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] Without a HALT, the DVG would keep trying to fetch and execute memory past the end of the list,
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] producing garbage or possibly crashing the hardware. The HALT provides a clean way to end each
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] frame and synchronise with the CPU.
2026-08-15 07:10:46 [deepseek-v4-flash] 
