2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] Yes, moving to a multidimensional or structured `forms` field would be highly beneficial,
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] especially for an assembler or disassembler.
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] A flat array of strings (like `"ED 4A"`) tells you the opcode bytes, but it doesn't tell you what
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] operands the instruction expects, how many bytes the total instruction takes, or where the dynamic
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] values (like `n`, `nn`, or `d`) fit into the byte sequence.
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] By making `forms` an array of objects, you can map the opcode directly to its syntax and structure.
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] This makes the JSON self-documenting and much easier to parse programmatically.
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] Here is an example of how you might structure it using a multidimensional approach:
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] "ADC": {
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 	"label": "add with carry",
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 	"flags": "SZHPNC",
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 	"forms": [
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		{
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"opcodes": ["CE"],
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"syntax": "ADC A,n",
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"length": 2,
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"operands": ["A", "n"]
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		},
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		{
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"opcodes": ["88"],
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"syntax": "ADC A,B",
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"length": 1,
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"operands": ["A", "B"]
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		},
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		{
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"opcodes": ["ED", "4A"],
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"syntax": "ADC HL,BC",
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"length": 2,
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"operands": ["HL", "BC"]
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		},
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 		{
2026-08-16 07:08:10 [glm-5.2] 			"opcodes": ["DD", "8E"],
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 			"syntax": "ADC A,(IX+d)",
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 			"length": 3,
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 			"operands": ["A", "(IX+d)"]
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 		}
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 	]
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] }
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] • Benefits of this approach:*
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 1. Explicit Operand Mapping: If you are writing an assembler, parsing `ADC A, B` means you
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] just look for a form where `operands` matches `["A", "B"]`. You instantly get your opcodes.
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 2. Instruction Length: The `length` field tells the disassembler exactly how many bytes to
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] read from memory, which is crucial for handling prefixed instructions properly (e.g., `DD 8E d` is
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 3 bytes, while `8E` is 1 byte).
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 3. Dynamic Value Placement: If you eventually add fields to define where the `d`, `n`, or
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] `nn` values are inserted into the opcode stream, having a structured object makes this much easier.
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] This structure keeps all the logic for a single mnemonic contained within its own block, rather
2026-08-16 07:08:11 [glm-5.2] than having to cross-reference back to the flat `encoding` table.
