2026-08-16 07:09:45 [nitro@ryzen5] i prefer space sepearated string over arrays for the inner fields, what are pros and cons?
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.018 29.59KB 17.06s
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] Using space-separated strings instead of arrays is a perfectly valid approach, especially for a
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] specification file that might be manually edited or read frequently. Both methods have their
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] trade-offs depending on how your parser is built.
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] Here are the pros and cons of using space-separated strings for fields like `"opcodes"` or
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] `"operands"`:
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] Pros of Space-Separated Strings
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] 1. Readability and Compactness: It visually closer matches how opcodes are typically
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] written in documentation (e.g., `"ED 4A"`). It reduces JSON clutter (no extra quotes or brackets),
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] making the file less noisy.
2026-08-16 07:10:02 [glm-5.2] 2. Ease of Manual Editing: If you are hand-typing this JSON, typing `"DD 8E"` is faster and
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] less error-prone than typing `["DD", "8E"]`.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 3. Slightly Smaller File Size: While negligible for a single CPU spec, removing the array
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] brackets and extra quotes does reduce the overall byte count.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 4. Direct String Matching: If you are disassembling, you can read bytes from memory,
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] convert them to a hex string, and do a direct lookup against the string (e.g., comparing `"ED4A"`
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] or `"ED 4A"`).
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] Cons of Space-Separated Strings
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 1. Parsing Overhead: Every time your engine reads an entry, it has to split the string
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] (e.g., `"ED 4A".split(" ")`) to get the individual bytes. With an array, the JSON parser already
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] did this for you.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 2. Fragility to Typos: It is easy to accidentally introduce double spaces (`"ED  4A"`) or
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] trailing spaces. If your parser uses strict splitting, this will cause lookup failures unless you
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] also trim and filter empty strings.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 3. Schema Validation: If you ever use tools like JSON Schema to validate your file,
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] validating that a string contains exactly two space-separated hex bytes is much harder than
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] validating an array of two hex strings.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 4. Ambiguity in Operands: For `"operands"`, a string like `"A (IX+d)"` is fine, but if you
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] ever need to represent multiple distinct operands as a list, you lose the clear semantic boundary
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] that an array provides.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] Summary
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] If your primary goal is human readability and the JSON is meant to be a clean reference
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] document that your engine parses once at startup, space-separated strings are an excellent choice.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] If your primary goal is machine processing and strict validation, arrays are generally
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] safer.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] Given your current `encoding` block already uses string keys for opcodes (e.g., `"ED 4A"`),
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] sticking with space-separated strings in the `forms` object will keep your data style consistent.
2026-08-16 07:10:03 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:07 [nitro@ryzen5] will i need to  multiple distinct operands as a list
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.020 32.38KB 18.29s
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] You will likely not need to use a list for operands in the Z80, because the architecture
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] has a strict limit of two operands per instruction (e.g., `LD B, C` or `BIT 3, B`).
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] If you prefer strings, you can simply use a comma to separate them, which naturally matches
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] standard assembly syntax.
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] For example:
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] {
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 	"opcodes": "88",
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 	"syntax": "ADC A,B",
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 	"operands": "A,B"
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] }
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] • When a string works perfectly:*
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] As long as your operand tokens do not contain spaces, a comma-separated string is very easy for a
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] parser to split (e.g., `operands.split(",")`). Tokens like `B`, `(HL)`, `(IX+d)`, and `n` are all
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] single words, so this approach is clean and reliable.
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] • The one caveat to watch for:*
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] If you ever define operands that contain spaces, such as writing `(IX + d)` instead of `(IX+d)`,
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] splitting the string becomes slightly more complex because you have to parse the parentheses rather
2026-08-16 07:11:26 [glm-5.2] than just splitting by comma. As long as you enforce a "no spaces inside operands" rule in your
