2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.019 37.99KB 7.44s
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] Yes, that covers all of the officially documented forms of the `ADC` instruction for the Z80.
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] To summarize, you have:
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] • `ADC A, r` (8-bit register to accumulator)
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] • `ADC A, (HL)` (8-bit memory to accumulator)
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] • `ADC A, n` (8-bit immediate to accumulator)
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] • `ADC A, (IX+d)` / `ADC A, (IY+d)` (8-bit indexed memory to accumulator)
2026-08-16 07:15:14 [glm-5.2] • `ADC HL, rr` (16-bit register to HL)
2026-08-16 07:15:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:15:15 [glm-5.2] The only thing not included here are the undocumented `ADC A, IXH`/`IXL`/`IYH`/`IYL` variations
2026-08-16 07:15:15 [glm-5.2] (often referred to as the `H` and `L` halves of the index registers). If you are strictly sticking
2026-08-16 07:15:15 [glm-5.2] to the documented Z80 specification, your list is complete.
2026-08-16 07:15:15 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:15:42 [nitro@ryzen5] it could be object, is ordering array form a suitable idea?
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.024 38.87KB 17.72s
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] Using an array for the `forms` is a very suitable idea, especially if order matters to you.
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] However, switching to an object keyed by the opcode string is also an excellent approach and aligns
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] closely with how you structured your main `encoding` block.
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] Here is a comparison of the two approaches for your `forms` field:
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 1. The Array Form (Current)
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] "forms": [
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 	{ "opcodes": "8F", "syntax": "ADC A,A" },
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 	{ "opcodes": "88", "syntax": "ADC A,B" }
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] ]
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • Pros:*
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • Ordering: You can explicitly group and order the forms (e.g., listing all `ADC A,r` forms
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] first, then `ADC A,n`, then `ADC HL,rr`). This is nice for human readability and documentation
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] generation.
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • Extensibility: If you ever decide to add metadata back to specific forms (like cycle
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] counts or undocumented flags), you already have objects in place to do so.
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • Cons:*
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • More verbose. Your parser has to iterate through the array to find a matching opcode.
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 2. The Object Form (Keyed by Opcodes)
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] "forms": {
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 	"8F": "ADC A,A",
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 	"88": "ADC A,B",
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 	"DD 8E": "ADC A,(IX+d)",
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 	"ED 4A": "ADC HL,BC"
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] }
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • Pros:*
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] • Extremely Compact: Removes the need for nested objects and repeated `opcodes`/`syntax`
2026-08-16 07:16:01 [glm-5.2] keys.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] • Fast Lookups: If you are disassembling, you read the bytes from memory, construct the key
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] string (e.g., `"DD 8E"`), and instantly access the syntax string via `instruction.forms[key]`.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] • Guarantees Uniqueness: An object naturally prevents you from accidentally defining the
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] same opcode twice.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] • Cons:*
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] • No Guaranteed Order: While modern JSON parsers typically preserve insertion order, JSON
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] specification itself does not guarantee object key ordering. If you rely on a specific sequence,
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] this could be a minor issue.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] • Less Extensible: If you want to add cycle counts later, you'd have to change the value
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] from a string back to an object, like `"8F": { "syntax": "ADC A,A", "cycles": 4 }`.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] Summary
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] If your goal is maximum compactness and direct opcode-to-syntax mapping (which is great for a
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] disassembler), the Object Form keyed by opcodes is arguably the better choice.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] If you value strict ordering and might want to add per-form metadata later without changing the
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] structure, the Array Form is the way to go.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] Given your preference for compactness and space-separated strings shown so far, the object form
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] would look remarkably clean.
2026-08-16 07:16:02 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:19 [nitro@ryzen5] can cycles be deduced from address mode alone?
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.2 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.023 41.56KB 16.05s
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] No, cycles cannot be deduced from the address mode alone. While the address mode is a major factor,
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] the timing in a Z80 is determined by a combination of the instruction being executed, the specific
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] registers involved, and the address mode.
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] Here are a few examples from the official Z80 timing tables that demonstrate why address mode alone
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] isn't enough:
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] • 1. Same Address Mode, Different Instructions*
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] If we look at the "Register Indirect" address mode using `(HL)`:
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] • `LD A, (HL)` takes 7 clock cycles (T-states).
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] • `INC (HL)` takes 11 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] • `RLC (HL)` (via the CB prefix) takes 15 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] The CPU has to do different amounts of work (fetching, executing, writing back) depending on what
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] the instruction actually does, even if the way it gets the data is the same.
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] • 2. Same Address Mode, Different Registers*
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] If we look at the "Extended" address mode (reading from a specific 16-bit memory address `nn`):
2026-08-16 07:17:35 [glm-5.2] • `LD A, (nn)` takes 13 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • `LD HL, (nn)` takes 16 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • `LD BC, (nn)` takes 20 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] Even though the address mode is identical, moving data into an 8-bit accumulator is faster than
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] moving 16-bit data into a register pair, and HL has dedicated hardware shortcuts that BC/DE/SP do
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] not, making HL operations slightly faster.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • 3. Same Address Mode, Different Contexts*
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] In the "Register" address mode:
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • `LD B, C` takes 4 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • `ADD HL, BC` takes 11 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • `EX DE, HL` takes 4 clock cycles.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] • Conclusion for your JSON spec:*
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] If you plan to include cycle counts (often split into M-cycles and T-states for accurate Z80
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] emulation), you will need to store them at the individual form level. You cannot reliably calculate
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] them just by looking up a property on the address mode.
2026-08-16 07:17:36 [glm-5.2] 
