2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] That rule is actually not universal. While `ADD HL, rr` (and `ADD IX/IY, rr`) only affects `HNC`,
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] the other 16-bit arithmetic instructions behave differently:
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] • `ADC HL, rr` affects `SZHPNC` (it updates Sign, Zero, and Overflow).
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] • `SBC HL, rr` affects `SZHPNC`.
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] • `INC rr` and `DEC rr` affect no flags at all.
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] Because `ADC` is consistent across all its 8-bit and 16-bit forms (`SZHPNC`), it can safely stay at
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] the instruction level. `ADD`, however, is a split case.
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] If you prefer to keep the top-level `flags` and handle the 8-bit vs 16-bit logic in your engine,
2026-08-16 07:22:27 [glm-5.2] here is the `ADD` block without the inner `flags` payload:
