2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] Thank you for sharing the updated file. I see what you did with the `BIT` instruction—that is a
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] very clever optimization!
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] Instead of writing out all 80 forms for bits 0 through 7, you used `b` as a variable in the syntax
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] (e.g., `"BIT b,B"`) and mapped it to just the base opcodes for the registers. This saves a massive
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] amount of space in the JSON file.
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] There is one important engine design consideration to keep in mind with this approach:
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] • Parsing Logic Shift*
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] Because you are using a variable `b` and a base opcode (e.g., `CB 40`), your engine can no longer
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] do a direct 1:1 string lookup for `CB` instructions.
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] • If your disassembler reads the bytes `CB 52` from memory, it won't find `"CB 52"` in your `forms`
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] object.
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] • Your engine will need to implement masking logic: it will have to mask the second byte (`0x52 &
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 0xC0 == 0x40`), realize it's a `BIT` instruction, look up the base form `CB 40`, and then extract
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] the bit number by shifting right (`(0x52 >> 3) & 0x07 == 2`) to replace the `b` variable in the
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] syntax string.
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] • Similarly, your assembler will have to parse `BIT 2, D`, recognize the `b` variable, and
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] construct the final opcode by OR-ing the base (`CB 42`) with the bit number shifted into place (`2
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] << 3`).
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] This is a very common and highly efficient way to implement CPU tables, but it does mean your `CB`
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] prefix handler will need to be slightly smarter than a simple dictionary lookup.
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] Are you planning to use this same `b` variable pattern for `RES` and `SET` when you get to them?
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] They follow the exact same encoding format.
2026-08-16 07:49:04 [glm-5.2] 
