2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] Yes, using a custom font in both Windows and macOS terminals is straightforward and generally
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] involves two main steps: installing the font onto your system, and then selecting it in your
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] terminal's settings.
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] Here is a brief overview of how the process works on each platform:
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] macOS (Terminal.app or iTerm2)
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Install the font: Download your desired font file (`.ttf` or `.otf`) and open it with
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] Font Book, then click Install Font.
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Apply the font:
2026-08-17 08:14:15 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * In Terminal, open Settings (or Preferences) \rightarrow
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] Profiles \rightarrow Text, click the Change button under the Font section,
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] and choose your installed font.
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * In iTerm2, navigate to Settings \rightarrow Profiles \rightarrow
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] Text and select the font from the drop-down menu.
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] Windows (Windows Terminal)
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Install the font: Right-click the downloaded font file (`.ttf` or `.otf`) and choose
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] Install or Install for all users.
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Apply the font:
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Open Windows Terminal, open Settings, and select your default profile (or a
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] specific profile like PowerShell or Command Prompt).
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Go to Appearance, and select your font from the Font face drop-down list.
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] Key Consideration
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Terminals generally require or strongly prefer monospaced fonts (such as Fira
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] Code, JetBrains Mono, or Cascadia Code). While some terminals allow
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] proportional fonts, monospaced fonts ensure that columns, tables, and command line tools align
2026-08-17 08:14:16 [gemini-3.7-flash] correctly.
