2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] There are two distinct reasons `0xA0` and `0xA1` might fail depending on how your code handles
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] input:
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] Reason A: `0xA0`/`0xA1` vs. `0x10` (`VK_SHIFT`)
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] If your keyboard input listener listens to standard browser events (`keydown`/`keyup`) or standard
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] Win32 `WM_KEYDOWN` messages:
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] • The system sends `0x10` (`VK_SHIFT`) by default for both shift keys, not `0xA0`
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] or `0xA1`.
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `0xA0` (`VK_LSHIFT`) and `0xA1` (`VK_RSHIFT`) are only emitted if you use low-level keyboard
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] hooks or specifically translate them.
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Fix: If your input handler sends `0x10`, but your matrix only has `0xA0` and `0xA1`, the
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] Shift key will never match.
2026-08-17 09:07:07 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
