2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] Testing bit 15 (the most significant bit / `0x8000`) of the return value from
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] `GetAsyncKeyState` is the correct and standard way to determine if a key is currently held down.
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] Why this is effective for your matrix:
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Left vs. Right Support:
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash]    Unlike standard window messages, `GetAsyncKeyState` properly distinguishes between left and
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] right modifier keys. This means `VK_LSHIFT` (`0xA0`), `VK_RSHIFT` (`0xA1`), `VK_LCONTROL` (`0xA2`),
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] and `VK_LMENU` (`0xA4` / Left Alt) will all work independently when queried with this function.
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Standard Implementation Check:
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash]    Since `GetAsyncKeyState` returns a 16-bit signed integer (`SHORT`), the most reliable checks
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] are:
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Bitmask: `(GetAsyncKeyState(vKey) & 0x8000) != 0`
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Sign comparison: `GetAsyncKeyState(vKey) < 0`
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] • (Note: You can safely ignore bit 0 of the return value, as its state tracking for whether the key
2026-08-17 09:18:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] was pressed since the last query is unreliable across modern Windows versions).*
