2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] In the Commodore PET ROM interrupt routine, `0104,X` is an indexed access into Page 1 (the 6502
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] hardware stack) to inspect the saved Processor Status register (P$).
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] How the Stack is Structured
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] When a hardware interrupt or a `BRK` occurs, the following sequence takes place:
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. The 6502 hardware automatically pushes:
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Program Counter High (`PC_hi`)
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Program Counter Low (`PC_lo`)
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Processor Status (`P`)
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. The ROM IRQ service routine immediately preserves registers:
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Pushes `A`
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Pushes `X`
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Pushes `Y`
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. It copies the stack pointer with `TSX` and executes `LDA $0104,X`.
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] Because the stack pointer points to the next empty location, offset `$0104,X` resolves directly to
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] the saved Processor Status byte pushed during step 1:
2026-08-18 07:12:42 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$0101,X` = Saved `Y`
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$0102,X` = Saved `X`
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$0103,X` = Saved `A`
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$0104,X` = Saved `P` (Status Register)
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$0105,X` = Saved `PC_lo`
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `$0106,X` = Saved `PC_hi`
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] Why the Check Happens
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] Both hardware `IRQ`s and the software `BRK` instruction share the interrupt vector at `FFFE/FFFF`.
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] The ROM checks the pushed status byte to determine which event occurred:
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Hardware IRQ: Pushes P with Bit 4 (B flag) = 0 and Bit 5 = 1.
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] • BRK / PHP: Pushes P with Bit 4 (B flag) = 1 and Bit 5 = 1.
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] The ROM executes `AND #$10` (checking bit 4) to branch to the monitor/break handler if `BRK` was
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] executed, or proceed with timer and keyboard scanning if it was a hardware IRQ.
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] Likely Cause of the Crash
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] If your simulator sets Bit 4 to 1 when pushing the status register during a hardware `IRQ`,
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] the PET kernel misidentifies the timer interrupt as a software break (`BRK`), redirecting execution
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] into the machine language monitor or warm-start routine rather than returning with `RTI`.
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] Ensuring that hardware interrupts clear Bit 4 when pushing the status register to the stack should
2026-08-18 07:12:43 [gemini-3.7-flash] resolve the issue.
