2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] This snippet is from the screen initialization and diagnostic configuration routine during
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] the PET kernel boot sequence (specifically from PET BASIC 2.0 / 4.0 ROMs, commonly referred to as
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] the EDIT ROM / Screen initialization).
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] Here is a breakdown of what each section is doing:
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. `$0224` Check: Screen Line Length
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `LDA 0224` / `BNE E6B0`:
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash]   Location `0224` is used to store the screen width configuration. If it has already been set (in
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] your trace, it holds `0C`), the branch skips an earlier default setup block and jumps directly to
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] `E6B0`.
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. State & Buffer Initialization
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `LDX #FF` / `STX 0223`:
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash]   Initializes the auto-repeat/key debounce flag or quote-mode state tracker to inactive (`$FF`).
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `INX` (`X` becomes `00`) / `STX 0204`:
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash]   Clears the keyboard buffer index / count (stored at `$0204`), ensuring the keyboard buffer starts
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] empty.
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:36 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Screen Height & Hardware Configuration (Diagnostic Pins)
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `LDX #$50` (`80` decimal):
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash]   Loads the number of characters for screen wrap / scroll line limits.
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `LDA E810` / `AND #F0` / `STA $E810`:
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Read (`LDA $E810`): Reads the current state of PIA 1 Port A. The upper 4 bits
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] (`PA4–PA7`) are wired to hardware configuration jumpers (diagnostic lines / user port / video
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] options).
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Mask (`AND #$F0`): Preserves the upper 4 diagnostic/configuration bits while clearing
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] the lower 4 bits (which connect to the keyboard matrix drive/sense).
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Write (`STA $E810`): Writes the preserved upper bits back to the port output register
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] or configures the port lines before starting the main RAM memory test and screen RAM clear loop.
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 4. Preparing for the Screen Clear Loop
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `LDY #$00`:
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash]   Sets up the index register to begin zeroing/clearing the screen RAM starting at `$8000`.
2026-08-18 07:24:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:27 [nitro@ryzen5] how should my sim treat a write to pia1_pa
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.007 7.121KB 10.99s
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] In your simulator, handling a write to `$E810` (PIA 1, Port A) depends on standard 6520 PIA
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] register addressing and how the PET hardware is wired.
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Check the PIA Control Register (`$E811`)
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] The 6520 PIA uses bit 2 of its Control Register A (`CRA` at `E811`) to determine whether register
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] `E810` targets the Data Direction Register or the Output Register:
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] • If `CRA` Bit 2 is `0`: The write updates `DDRA` (Data Direction Register A).
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * A `0` bit configures the corresponding pin as an input.
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * A `1` bit configures the corresponding pin as an output.
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * During normal PET operation, the ROM sets `DDRA` to `$00` (all 8 lines as inputs).
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] • If `CRA` Bit 2 is `1`: The write updates `ORA` (Output Register A).
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Stores the byte in the internal output register latch.
2026-08-18 07:25:38 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. External Hardware Effects on the PET
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] • No Peripheral Side-Effects: Unlike PIA 1 Port B (which drives the keyboard row decoder)
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] or PIA 2 / VIA (which drive video, sound, and IEEE-488), PIA 1 Port A lines are wired only to the
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] keyboard matrix columns via pull-up resistors.
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Latch Storage Only: When the PET ROM writes to `$E810` while configured as outputs or
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] during initialization, you only need to store the written byte into the internal `ORA` state
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] variable. No external components need to be notified.
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Impact on Subsequent Reads
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] When the 6502 reads `$E810` (with `CRA` bit 2 = 1):
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] • For any bit where `DDRA` is `0` (Input): Return the keyboard line state (defaults to `1`
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] from pull-ups; goes to `0` if a key on the active row is closed).
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] • For any bit where `DDRA` is `1` (Output): Return the bit value previously stored in
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] `ORA`.
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] In practice, since the PET kernel configures all Port A pins as inputs (`DDRA = $00`), reads will
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] almost always reflect the keyboard matrix rather than the value written to `ORA`.
2026-08-18 07:25:39 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
