2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.002 11.08KB 2.30s
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] Screen memory on the Commodore PET is located at `8000`–`83E7` (40 columns × 25 lines =
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1,000 bytes) or `8000`–`87CF` on 80-column models (80 × 25 = 2,000 bytes).
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] Key Memory Map Details
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Start Address: `$8000`
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] • End Address (40-column PET): `$83E7` (1,000 characters)
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] • End Address (80-column PET): `$87CF` (2,000 characters)
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Total Address Space Allocated: `8000`–`87FF` (2 KB of static RAM wired to the video
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] display circuitry)
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] Organization
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:23 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Linear Layout: Screen RAM is mapped contiguously from top-left to bottom-right:
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * `$8000`: Row 0, Column 0 (top-left corner)
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * `$8027`: Row 0, Column 39 (top-right on 40-col models)
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * `$8028`: Row 1, Column 0
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * `$83E7`: Row 24, Column 39 (bottom-right on 40-col models)
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Screen Codes (Not ASCII/PETSCII):
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash]    The bytes in screen memory are PET Screen Codes, which map directly to character glyphs
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] in the character ROM (e.g., Screen Code `00` = `@`, `01` = `A`, `20` = Space, `80`–`$FF` = Inverted
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] characters).
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. No Color RAM:
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash]    Unlike the VIC-20 or C64, standard PET hardware is monochrome and has no separate Color RAM
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] area. Characters are displayed directly using only the byte at `$8000` + offset.
2026-08-18 08:49:24 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:03:21 [nitro@ryzen5] is commodore pet monochrome only?
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.003 498B 4.41s
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] Yes, the Commodore PET was monochrome only.
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] All production models in the PET (and later CBM) series were designed with integrated monochrome
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] displays and lacked native hardware support for color graphics.
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:03:26 [gemini-3.7-flash] Depending on the specific model and production run, the displays used different phosphor colors:
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] • White Phosphor: Used in the earliest PET 2001 models (9-inch integrated screens).
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Green Phosphor: The most common variety, found on later 9-inch and 12-inch models (such
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] as the 4000 and 8000 series).
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Amber Phosphor: Offered primarily in European markets for the later business models to
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] reduce eye strain.
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] The video hardware in the PET generated a single-bit digital video signal aimed directly at the
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] internal monitor circuitry (or an external monochrome monitor on models without a built-in screen).
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] While a few third-party expansion boards existed that could tap into the bus to output composite or
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] RGB color video, out of the box, the machine remained strictly monochrome. Commodore did not
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] introduce native color support in their personal computer line until the release of the VIC-20 in
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1980/1981.
2026-08-18 11:03:27 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:04:25 [nitro@ryzen5] so text was black on white unless reverse bit is on?
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.002 1.882KB 3.23s
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] It was actually the opposite by default: light text on a dark (black) background.
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] Here is how the display logic worked:
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Normal Mode (Default): The CRT screen background remained unlit (black), and the electron
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] beam lit up the individual phosphor pixels to draw the character. On a white-phosphor model, this
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] produced white text on a black background.
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Reverse Mode (Bit 7 Set): Setting the highest bit of a character's screen code inverted
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] the pixel output. This drew an illuminated solid block with the character itself carved out of
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] unlit (black) pixels, producing dark text on a bright rectangular background.
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] In BASIC, users could toggle this reverse state using the `RVS ON` and `RVS OFF` keys, or by
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] printing control codes such as `CHR(18)` (reverse on) and `CHR(146)` (reverse off).
2026-08-18 11:04:29 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
