2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. When Does PET Screen RAM Get Written?
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] The screen memory actually gets written in the next two stages:
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] A. The Clear-Screen Control Code
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] During the print sequence, the first character sent to `BSOUT` (`FFD2`) is PETSCII `93` (Clear
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] Screen / CLR).
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] • When the Screen Editor receives `93`, it runs a fast loop filling 1,000 bytes starting at `8000`
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] with `$20` (Space character code).
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] • This wipes out all the `$00` (`@`) bytes.
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] B. Writing Characters to Screen Codes
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] When text is finally streamed character-by-character through `BSOUT`:
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] • The screen editor takes each PETSCII byte (e.g., `'B'` = `42`), converts it to Screen Code (`'B'`
2026-08-18 18:10:46 [gemini-3.7-flash] = `02`), and writes it to `(E0),Y` (where `E0/E1` points to `8000 + (Row \times 40) + Col`).
