2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] Hardware Reason (Why Commodore PETs do this)
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Shared Video RAM Bus:
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * On early Commodore PET boards (original 2001 series and early upgrade boards), Video RAM
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] (`8000–83E7`) did not have dual-port arbiters.
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * If the 6502 CPU and the discrete video generator circuitry tried to access
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] video RAM at the exact same clock cycle, bus contention occurred. This appeared on the CRT display
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] as random white specks or flashes known as "snow."
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. The Fix:
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Pin 5 of VIA Port B (`PB5`) was wired directly to the video controller's Video Blanking /
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] Retrace signal.
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * During active beam scanning, Bit 5 is in one state. During the retrace blanking
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] interval (when the electron beam is shut off while resetting to draw the next scanline/frame),
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] Bit 5 toggles.
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * The Kernal checks this bit before executing `STA ($E0),Y` so CPU writes to screen RAM only
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] occur when the video beam is blanked—eliminating video snow entirely.
2026-08-18 18:15:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
