2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.010 4.106KB 10.00s
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] This behavior typically happens because PIA 1 Port B (`$E812`) is returning `0x00` instead of
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] `0xFF` when no keys are pressed.
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] Why This Happens in the PET ROM
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] The PET keyboard matrix uses open-collector lines pulled high by pull-up resistors:
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Column Drive (PIA 1 Port A / `E810`): The lower nibble drives a 74145 BCD-to-decimal
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] decoder to pull one of the 10 keyboard columns low (0–$9).
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Row Read (PIA 1 Port B / `$E812`): The 8 row lines are read as inputs.
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] The Kernal keyboard scan routine (`SCNKEY`) works like this:
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     LDX #$00        ; Start at Column 0
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] ScanLoop:
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     STX $E810       ; Output column index to 74145
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     LDA $E812       ; Read row lines (PIA1 Port B)
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     CMP #$FF        ; Are ALL lines pulled high (no key pressed)?
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     BNE KeyFound    ; If != 0xFF, a key is pressed -> exit loop!
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     INX
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     CPX #$0A        ; Done all 10 columns?
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash]     BNE ScanLoop
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] The Root Cause
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] • If your simulator returns `0x00` (default uninitialized memory) on reads to `E812`, the CPU
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] compares `00` against `$FF`.
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Because `00 != FF`, the Kernal believes every key in Column 0 is held down
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] simultaneously.
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] • It immediately branches out of the scanning loop on the first iteration without ever incrementing
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] to columns 1–9.
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] • At the end of the scan routine, the Kernal writes `F0` (an invalid BCD value > 9) to `E810` to
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] disable the 74145 decoder outputs.
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] How to Fix
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Default Floating State: Ensure reads from PIA 1 Port B (`$E812`) return `0xFF` when no
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] keys are pressed (active-low matrix).
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Key Press Representation: When a key is pressed at `(column, row)`, pulling that line
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] low should flip the corresponding bit to `0` (i.e., `0xFF & ~(1 << row)`).
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Data Direction Check: Verify that `E812` acts as input when Data Direction Register B
2026-08-19 08:25:18 [gemini-3.7-flash] (DDRB) is `00` (which the PET Kernal sets up during boot).
2026-08-19 08:25:19 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
