2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] Thanks — this is all new to me in this session, so a few observations across the three files.
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] • On the trace (notes.txt)*
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] The PET is alive and well: the IRQ chain (E66B → `JMP (0219)` → `INCTIME`) is ticking the jiffy
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] clock at 0205 (D3→D4→D5→D6→D7), the cursor blink countdown at 0225 resets to 14, and the keyboard
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] scan through PIA1_PA/PIA1_PB runs cleanly. The main thread, however, is parked in the polling loop
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] at E297–E29D waiting for 020D to go non-zero, which never happens. Since the keyboard scan reads FF
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] throughout, if 020D is the key buffer this is simply the machine idling for input — the emulator
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] side (`readPetMatrix()`) would need to return real key bits to break it.
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] One display convention worth knowing when reading these logs: in `branch()`, not-taken branches
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] print the fall-through PC with a trailing ``, not the actual target. So `BNE E68D` at E68B
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] is really "not taken, target was $E6B0". That makes the traces a little misleading at first glance.
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] • Possible bugs in machine.ts*
2026-08-19 12:02:14 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] - `epoch = Date.UTC(2026,10,8)` — JS months are 0-indexed, so this is 8 November 2026, not
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] 8 August as the comment states. Should be month `7`.
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] - In `tickVic()`, `0x83|(joyFire)?0:0x20|(sense)?0:0x40|((joyDir&7)<<2)` — the ternary binds looser
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] than `|`, so this parses as `(0x83|joyFire) ? ...`, which is always truthy. `$9111` therefore
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] always gets written as 0, breaking the VIC-20 joystick entirely. Needs parentheses.
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] - `onInput` case `49` logs `sabotage.title` but the import is `appleSabotage` — a ReferenceError
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] waiting to happen.
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] - Opcode `0xAF` is mapped as a 2-byte `LDA (zp,X)`, but the genuine article there is LAX absolute
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] (3 bytes, illegal opcode). Fine if intentional for a specific title, but it will silently
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] misexecute anything expecting real 0xAF semantics.
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] - `missingCodes` is a `Set`, but `memCode()` uses bracket assignment and `reportMissing()` uses
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] `Object.keys()` — it happens to work via accidental property assignment, but `.add()` would be the
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] honest version.
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] - Dead-code hazards: `refreshCRT()` references an undefined `game`, and `displayApple1()` tests an
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] undefined `frame`. Both would throw if ever called.
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] • Otherwise*
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] The architecture is a nice piece of work — a single 6502 core with per-platform bus interception,
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] ANSI/braille rendering, the DVG vector engine, and tristate WAV tape extraction all coexisting
2026-08-19 12:02:15 [glm-5.3] cleanly. Happy to dig into any of these areas further, or into why $020D never gets written.
