2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] • New regression in branch()*
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] The restructuring introduced a real bug. `const target=(pc + 2 + offset) & 0xFFFF` is now computed
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] before the `offset -= 256` sign adjustment, which only happens inside `if(taken)` — and
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] only feeds `branchBackwards()`. Since `get8()` returns an unsigned byte, every taken backward
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] branch now lands 256 bytes too high.
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] Using your own PET idle loop from the trace as the example: at `E29A`, `BEQ E297` encodes offset
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] `FB` (−5). The new code computes `pc[0] = E29C + FB = E397` instead of `E297` — so the very first
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] iteration of the main wait loop wanders off into `E397`. Forward branches are unaffected. The
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] remedy is simply to move the `target` computation back inside `if(taken)`, after the sign extension
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] (or sign-extend offset once, up front, and derive everything from that).
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] The associated display change is otherwise an improvement — returning the true target with `*`
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] marking not-taken is much clearer than the old fall-through-PC convention.
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:41:24 [glm-5.3] • Knock-on from the Set fix*
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] `reportMissing()` still uses `Object.keys(missingCodes)`, which returns an empty array for a Set —
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] so it now silently reports nothing. The old bracket assignment worked with `Object.keys()` by
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] accident; `.add()` doesn't. Iterating the Set directly (`for(const code of missingCodes)`) would
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] restore it.
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 12:41:25 [glm-5.3] • On 0xAF*
