2026-08-19 15:48:41 [nitro@ryzen5] with apple2plus the reset code does not set the stack ? maybe i am using wrong vector or there is alternative entry point?
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 glm-5.3 Z 🪠 0.7° $0.006 578B 853.02s
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] Apple II Plus Reset and Stack Setup
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] You're correct — the soft-entry vectors don't set the stack. Here's how it works:
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] Reset flow on the Apple II Plus
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 6502 RESET
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3]   → reads $FFFC/$FFFD → $FF59 (Monitor ROM)
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3]       → LDX #$FF / TXS          ← stack IS set here
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3]       → checks $3F4 (power-up byte vs $A5 xor hi-byte of $3F2)
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3]       → if match: JMP ($3F2)    ← soft reset vector (often $3D0 or $E000)
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3]       → if no match: cold start (RAM check, screen init, etc.)
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] What doesn't set the stack
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] If you're entering at any of these, the stack is not initialised:
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] - `3F2/3F3` — the soft reset vector itself (just an address)
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] - `$3D0` — DOS warm start
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] - `$E000` — Applesoft BASIC cold entry
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] - `00` / `01` — alternate Applesoft entries
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] What to use instead
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] ╭───────────────────┬──────────────┬─────────────────────────────────────────╮
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] │  Entry point      │  Sets stack  │  Notes                                  │
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] ├───────────────────┼──────────────┼─────────────────────────────────────────┤
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] │  **$FF59**        │  ✅           │  Monitor ROM reset — proper soft reset  │
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] │  **$FFFC/$FFFD**  │  ✅           │  Trigger actual 6502 RESET line         │
2026-08-19 16:02:54 [glm-5.3] │  $FAA6            │  ✅           │  Monitor cold-ish entry (also sets SP)  │
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] ╰───────────────────┴──────────────┴─────────────────────────────────────────╯
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] Recommendation
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] If you're emulating or calling a reset programmatically:
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] ; Either trigger hardware reset via the vector at $FFFC
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] ; or jump to the monitor entry:
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] JMP $FF59        ; soft reset, sets SP=$FF
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] If you're using an emulator API, look for a `reset()` or `coldReset()` method that goes through the
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] `FFFC` vector rather than jumping to `3F2` directly.
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] Does this match what you're seeing, or are you working with a specific emulator/library where the
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] behaviour differs?
2026-08-19 16:02:55 [glm-5.3] 
