2026-08-19 17:40:02 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * The selected row line is pulled LOW (0); all unselected rows remain HIGH (1).
2026-08-19 17:40:02 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * When a key is pressed, it shorts the selected low row line to the corresponding column line
2026-08-19 17:40:02 [gemini-3.7-flash] going into PIA1 Port B (`$E812`).
2026-08-19 17:40:02 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Matrix Output:
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Unpressed keys pull Port B HIGH (`0xFF`) via pull-up resistors.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Pressed keys pull the corresponding bit LOW (`0`).
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]   * Summary: A fully open/unpressed keyboard matrix must return `0xFF`, and a pressed key
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] pulls its bit to `0` (active low). If your matrix returns `0x00` when idle or `1` for pressed keys,
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] the ROM will either register every key pressed at once or ignore them completely.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Output Masking on Read of Port A (`$E810`)
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] In your current implementation:
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] case 0xe810: // PIA1_PA
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]     ...
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]     value |= 0xc0;
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]     break;
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] On the PET:
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Bits 0–3 are outputs (keyboard row drive).
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • When reading PRA back, the low 4 bits should reflect the value last written to `$E810`.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • If you overwrite or return an uninitialised `mem[address]`, the PET ROM's keyboard debounce
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] routines (which read `$E810` back to confirm the current row) might read corrupt data.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. `$E813` (CRB) CB1/CB2 Frame Blanking Interrupt
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] The PET keyboard scan is driven by an interrupt handler that triggers on the vertical retrace pulse
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] wired to PIA1 CB1:
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • On real hardware, the 60 Hz vertical blank pulse connects to PIA1 line CB1.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • This sets Bit 7 of `E813` (CRB) to `1` and triggers an IRQ (if bit 0 of `E813` is
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] enabled).
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Inside the PET Kernal IRQ routine:
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]   1. It checks if bit 7 of `$E813` is set.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]   2. It reads `E812` (PRB), which automatically clears bit 7 of `E813`.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash]   3. It executes the keyboard scan routine (`E680` on Basic 4 / `E4B0` on Basic 2).
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] If the IRQ is generated by a timer rather than asserting bit 7 of `$E813`, the ROM interrupt
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] handler will skip the keyboard scanning routine entirely.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] Summary Checklist for PET Keyboard
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Row Strobe ($E810): Bits 0–3 select row 0–9.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Column Return ($E812): Returns `0xFF` when no keys are pressed on that row; bits are
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] pulled to `0` for pressed keys.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Interrupt Flag ($E813): Bit 7 must be set at 50/60 Hz to signal vertical blank and
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] trigger the Kernal keyboard scan interrupt.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 4. Flag Clear on Read: Reading `E812` must clear bit 7 of `E813`.
2026-08-19 17:40:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:26 [nitro@ryzen5] is the row strobe triggered by just write or read also?
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.046 170.6KB 4.09s
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] The row strobe is driven strictly by writes to `E810` (PIA1 Port A). A read from `E810`
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] does not change the active row.
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] Hardware Flow
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Write to `$E810` (Row Select):
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * The 6502 writes a 4-bit value (0–9) to bits 0–3 of Port A.
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * This value is latched internally by the 6520 PIA and presented on output pins `PA0`–`PA3`.
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * These 4 lines feed directly into the 74145 BCD-to-Decimal decoder, which pulls the
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] chosen row low.
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Read from `$E812` (Column Sense):
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * The 6502 reads Port B to check which column lines have been pulled low by pressed keys on that
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] row.
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Read from `$E810` (Optional by software):
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * A read of `$E810` simply samples the PIA pins/output register and returns the value currently
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] on the lines (plus IEEE-488 / Cassette input bits on lines 6–7). It does not alter the latch or
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] change the selected row.
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] In Your Emulator Code
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] • You should only invoke `scanKeyboard(value & 0x0F)` inside `writePET()` when `address === 0xE810`
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] (and `cra & 4 !== 0`).
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] • `readPET(0xE810)` should only return the latched value and clear IRQ flags in `$E811` without
2026-08-19 17:43:31 [gemini-3.7-flash] triggering any row changes.
