2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] What This Means for Your Implementation
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Port Selection (`crb & 0x04`):
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Since bit 2 is `1` (`0x35 & 0x04 !== 0`), reading `$E812` correctly routes to
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] `readPetMatrix()` rather than returning `ddrb`.
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Interrupt Flag (`mem[0xE813] & 0x80`):
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * When vertical blank occurs, the PET expects bit 7 of `E813` to be set to 1 (making
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] `E813` read as `B5` or `80 | 0x35`).
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * The PET Kernal IRQ handler checks `LDA $E813`, tests bit 7, and if present, jumps into the
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] keyboard scan routine.
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. Clearing Bit 7:
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * When the ROM reads `$E812` during the scan, your code currently performs:
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash]      mem[0xE813] &= 0x3F;
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * This preserves the `$35` configuration while clearing the interrupt flag bit 7, matching
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] physical PIA1 behaviour.
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:56:28 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
