2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] The Problem: How `INC $E810` Works on 6502
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] The Commodore PET Kernal scans the keyboard rows using the `INC $E810` instruction inside a loop.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. A Read-Modify-Write cycle on the 6502:
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Reads `$E810` via `readPET()` / `readAddressBus()`.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Increments the value internally in the ALU.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Writes the result back via `writePET()` / `writeAddress()`.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. What your `readPET(0xE810)` was doing:
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    case 0xe810: // PIA1_PA
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]        ...
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]        mem[0xE811] &= 0x3f;
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]        value |= 0xc0;
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]        break;
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    Because `readPET()` returns `value | 0xC0`, the Kernal reads `C0`, `C1`, `C2`, ..., `C9`.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 3. What your `writeAddress()` was doing:
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    Inside `writeAddress()`:
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    if(isPET){
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]        if(block==0xe800){
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]            this.writePET(address,value);
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]            return;  // <--- RETURNING EARLY WITHOUT UPDATING mem[0xE810]!
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]        }
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    }
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    this.memory[address]=value;
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] Because `writeAddress` returned early without storing `value` into `this.memory[0xE810]`:
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] • When `writePET(0xE810, 0xC0)` ran initially, `mem[0xE810]` stayed `$00` (or old data).
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] • When `INC $E810` executed:
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]   1. `readPET(0xE810)` read `mem[0xE810]` (`00`), ORed `C0` → returned `$C0`.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]   2. `INC` computed `C0 + 1 = C1`.
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]   3. `writeAddress()` called `writePET(0xE810, C1)`, but did not store `C1` into
2026-08-19 17:59:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] `mem[0xE810]`.
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash]   4. On the next iteration, `readPET(0xE810)` read `mem[0xE810]` again — which was still `$00`!
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash]   5. The ROM loop was stuck scanning the same row repeatedly because `mem[0xE810]` was never
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] updated in memory.
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] The Fix
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] Make sure `mem[address] = value;` is updated in `writePET()` so that subsequent reads of `E810`
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] during `INC E810` see the incremented row count:
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] writePET(address: number, value: number) {
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 	const mem = this.memory;
2026-08-19 17:59:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] 	const label = this.addresLabel(address);
