2026-08-20 09:31:05 [nitro@ryzen5] on plus4 what is fd20 location
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 gemini-3.7-flash 🌟 🪠 0.7° $0.006 495B 7.50s
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] On the Commodore Plus/4 (and related 264-series machines such as the C16 and C116), address
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] `FD20` (64800 decimal) resides in the upper KERNAL ROM region (E000–$FFFF).
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] Context and Function
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Subsystem: Cassette (Datasette) Handling
2026-08-20 09:31:12 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Function: In the standard Plus/4 KERNAL ROM, `$FD20` is an internal routine used for
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] low-level tape I/O operations (part of the tape block read and header search routines).
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] Considerations
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] Because `FD20` is an internal KERNAL routine rather than a standardized jump table entry (which are
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] located from `FF81` to `FFF5`), its exact position and behaviour can vary across different KERNAL
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] revisions (e.g., PAL vs. NTSC revisions or custom ROMs). For cassette operations in software, it is
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] generally recommended to interface via the official KERNAL vectors (such as `LOAD` at `FFD5` or
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] `SAVE` at `$FFD8`).
2026-08-20 09:31:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
