2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] Yes, but with one important distinction regarding Bank 0:
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. Banks 1, 2, and 3 (Cartridges & 3+1 ROM)
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] You can safely treat these as open bus (or return unmapped/empty memory):
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • If `8007–8009` does not return `"CBM"`, the KERNAL simply skips the bank and moves to the next.
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] • This accurately simulates a machine without the "3+1" software ROMs or external cartridges
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] installed.
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 10:33:03 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Bank 0 (BASIC ROM)
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] You must map the standard BASIC ROM to `8000–BFFF` for Bank 0:
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] • The BASIC V3.5 interpreter code resides in the `8000–BFFF` address space.
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Once the cartridge scan finishes at Bank 0 (`FDD0`), the KERNAL transitions to initializing
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] BASIC. If `8000–$BFFF` is open bus at that point, the CPU will execute invalid opcodes and crash.
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] • The standard BASIC ROM does not have the `"CBM"` signature at `8007–8009`, so the header check
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] will naturally fail and boot will proceed normally into the BASIC prompt.
2026-08-20 10:33:04 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
