2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] Key Takeaways for Simulator Architecture
2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. The I/O Holes (`FD00–FDDF` and `FF00–FF3F`):
2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash]    Writes to these ranges are intercepted by the PLA and routed to on-board peripheral
2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] chips or bank-switching latches. They do not write to underlying RAM.
2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-20 10:54:13 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. The Vector / Trampoline Hole (`FF40–FFFF`):
2026-08-20 10:54:14 [gemini-3.7-flash]    This area at the very top of the memory map contains no I/O chips.
2026-08-20 10:54:14 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Reads return the KERNAL ROM (when ROM is enabled via `$FF3E`).
2026-08-20 10:54:14 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * Writes always pass through to underlying RAM.
2026-08-20 10:54:14 [gemini-3.7-flash]    * This is why the loop in your trace (`LDA FFF6,X` \rightarrow `STA FFF6,X`) successfully clones
2026-08-20 10:54:14 [gemini-3.7-flash] the ROM interrupt and reset vectors into RAM.
