2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.015 49.58KB 50.56s
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] I’ve reviewed the files under `C:\nitrologic\gadget`. The project is an interesting cross-platform
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] ANSI desktop manager built on Deno, with a modular FFI layer for keyboard, mouse, joystick, MIDI,
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] and audio. The code is well‑structured for a prototype, but I’ve noticed several issues that could
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] affect reliability, especially on macOS and Windows.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Key observations:
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Architecture
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   `terminal.ts` provides a unified API on top of platform‑specific backends (Windows vs. macOS).
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] This is a solid pattern for Deno FFI work. The separation of audio, MIDI, and input handling is
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] clear.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Mouse button handling
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   In `pollKeypad`, the SGR mouse sequence toggles button bits (`mouseButtons^=1`) instead of
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] explicitly setting/clearing them based on `M` (press) vs. `m` (release). This can cause the button
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] state to become inconsistent if presses/releases are missed or repeated.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - macOS MIDI
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   `macosmidi.ts` sets up a callback that only logs to the console via `console.log`, and
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `pollMidi()` always returns an empty array. As a result, MIDI events never appear in the
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `KeypadState.midiEvents` — making MIDI non‑functional from the application’s perspective.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Windows MIDI cleanup
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   In `windowsmidi.ts`, the `closeMidi` function uses `for(const midiOut in
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Object.values(midiOutputs))`. `for...in` iterates over keys (strings), not values, so
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `midiOut` becomes a string rather than a pointer. This will cause type errors or undefined
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] behaviour when passed to `midiOutClose`.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - macOS audio failure (`-50`)
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   The `AudioQueueNewOutput` failure is likely due to one or more of these factors:
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   - The callback (`noOpCallback`) is not marked `threadSafe`. AudioQueues invoke callbacks on a
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] background thread, so a plain `UnsafeCallback` may not be valid in that context. Use
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `Deno.UnsafeCallback.threadSafe` instead.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   - The `AudioStreamBasicDescription` may need native‑endian flags or a different format alignment
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] depending on the system’s audio hardware.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   - Starting the queue immediately after creation (before enqueuing any buffers) can also cause a
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] parameter error on some systems.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Audio resource management
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   Both `macosaudio.ts` and `windowsaudio.ts` leak memory: every `playAudio` call allocates
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] buffers/headers that are only stored in an array and never released. Over time, this will exhaust
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] memory. Proper cleanup (unprepare headers, dispose buffers, close queues) is needed.
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Gadget UI skeleton
2026-08-26 15:55:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   `gadget.ts` currently doesn’t render anything – `updateConsole` is empty, and the `panel` class
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] is unused. The desktop manager concept is clear, but the drawing/gadget layer is not yet
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] implemented.
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Minor notes
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision]   The `KeyBits` enum comments don’t match the actual numeric assignments (TypeScript enums start at
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 0, not 1/2/4/8). The comment block is misleading. Also, `QToQuit` is hard‑coded to `false`, so the
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `q` key quit path is never active.
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Overall, the project has a strong foundation but needs a few fixes before it can be considered
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] stable across platforms. If you’d like to dig into any specific issue (e.g., the macOS audio error
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] or the MIDI callback), I’m happy to help with a focused discussion.
2026-08-26 15:55:45 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:38 [nitro@ryzen5] if mixing wide and normal unicode chars should i use my own isWide method?
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.010 53.05KB 19.60s
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Yes, you should absolutely implement your own width‑detection logic rather than relying on
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `String.length` or a simple `isWide` boolean. For a desktop manager that draws windows, borders,
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] and text, you need to know exactly how many terminal cells a character (or grapheme cluster)
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] occupies. A single boolean isn’t enough because:
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Wide chars (CJK, emoji) take 2 cells.
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Narrow chars (ASCII, most punctuation) take 1 cell.
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Zero‑width chars (combining marks, ZWJ, variation selectors) take 0 cells.
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] If you mix these, cursor positioning, box drawing, and text alignment will all break.
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The good news: you don’t need a perfect implementation for a prototype. A reasonable approximation
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] based on Unicode ranges will get you 95% of the way. Here’s a compact `charWidth` function you can
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] drop into your project:
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:58 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] export function charWidth(ch: string): number {
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	const code = ch.codePointAt(0)!;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Control characters
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (code < 32 || (code >= 0x7F && code <= 0x9F)) return 0;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Combining diacritics, variation selectors, etc.
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x0300 && code <= 0x036F) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x1AB0 && code <= 0x1AFF) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x1DC0 && code <= 0x1DFF) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x20D0 && code <= 0x20FF) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0xFE20 && code <= 0xFE2F)
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	) return 0;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// East Asian Wide / Fullwidth
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x1100 && code <= 0x115F) || // Hangul Jamo
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x2E80 && code <= 0xA4CF) || // CJK radicals etc.
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0xAC00 && code <= 0xD7A3) || // Hangul syllables
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0xF900 && code <= 0xFAFF) || // CJK compatibility
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0xFE30 && code <= 0xFE4F) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0xFF00 && code <= 0xFF60) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0xFFE0 && code <= 0xFFE6)
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	) return 2;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Emoji blocks
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x1F300 && code <= 0x1FAFF) ||
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		(code >= 0x2600 && code <= 0x27BF)
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	) return 2;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	return 1;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] }
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] You can then write a `stringWidth` helper that sums widths across code points:
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] export function stringWidth(text: string): number {
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	let width = 0;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	for (const ch of Array.from(text)) {
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		width += charWidth(ch);
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	}
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	return width;
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] }
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] • Caveats:*
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Grapheme clusters (e.g., an emoji with skin tone, or a family emoji made of ZWJ
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] sequences) are more complex. You can either iterate by code point as above, or use
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `Intl.Segmenter("en", {granularity: "grapheme"})` to handle clusters, then sum the widths. But for
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] most UI work, the simple code‑point loop is sufficient.
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Ambiguous width characters (like box‑drawing `━`, `╭`, etc.) are technically “East Asian
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Ambiguous” – their width depends on the terminal’s locale (often 1 in Western locales, 2 in CJK
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] locales). Most modern terminals treat them as 1 cell, and your solution should too. The function
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] above deliberately treats them as 1.
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Zero‑width joiner (ZWJ) and variation selectors aren’t handled in the simple version. If
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] you need precise emoji rendering, you’ll need to skip `0x200D` (ZWJ) and `0xFE0E`/`0xFE0F`
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] (variation selectors) as well.
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] For a serious project, consider using a well‑tested library like
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] [`string-width`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/string-width) (via `npm:` imports in Deno) – it’s
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] battle‑tested and handles more edge cases. But if you want to stay dependency‑free, the function
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] above is a solid starting point.
2026-08-26 16:04:59 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:05:00 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Since you’re building an ANSI desktop manager, I’d recommend wrapping this logic in your
2026-08-26 16:05:00 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `gadget.ts` so all drawing functions can use `stringWidth` instead of `.length` when calculating
2026-08-26 16:05:00 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] padding, alignment, and cursor positions. This will prevent the “broken box” artifacts that appear
2026-08-26 16:05:00 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] when wide characters are placed inside narrow cells.
2026-08-26 16:05:00 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
