2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Now let’s implement real grapheme cluster support. First, a crucial point: you cannot know from
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] JavaScript alone how a terminal will render a grapheme cluster. The width is determined by the
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] terminal’s font and its shaping engine. VS Code’s editor and terminal are different components —
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] the editor has its own rendering system, while the terminal relies on the terminal emulator (like
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] xterm.js, Windows Terminal, etc.). So yes, a family emoji like `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦` may render as one
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] glyph in the editor but as a sequence of four emoji in the terminal.
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] For this reason, we need a pragmatic approach: use a heuristic that matches the dominant behavior
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] of modern terminals, while allowing easy adjustment if your target terminal behaves differently.
2026-08-26 16:14:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:14:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Cluster support with `Intl.Segmenter`
2026-08-26 16:14:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:14:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Deno (via V8) supports `Intl.Segmenter`. We’ll use it to split text into true grapheme clusters,
2026-08-26 16:14:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] then estimate the width of each cluster.
