2026-08-26 16:16:51 [nitro@ryzen5] is mode 2027 in ansi something we should discuss
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.018 110.8KB 19.94s
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Good question — mode 2027 is directly relevant to grapheme cluster handling, so yes, we should
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] discuss it.
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] What is ANSI mode 2027?
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Mode 2027 is a DEC private mode (like 1006 for mouse, 1004 for focus events) that controls
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] grapheme cluster handling in terminals. When enabled, the terminal treats each Unicode
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] grapheme cluster (e.g., `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦`, `é` = `e` + combining acute, `🇺🇸` = regional indicators)
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] as a single indivisible unit for cursor movement, backspacing, and display width.
2026-08-26 16:17:11 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The escape sequences are:
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] const GRAPHEME_MODE_ON  = "\x1b[?2027h";
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] const GRAPHEME_MODE_OFF = "\x1b[?2027l";
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Why it matters for your desktop manager
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Currently your `graphemeWidth()` function makes an educated guess that a ZWJ emoji sequence
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] occupies 2 cells. If the terminal supports mode 2027, that guess becomes exactly
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] correct — the terminal will render the cluster as a single wide character instead of a sequence
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] of separate emoji.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] This alignment is important because:
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - If your `stringWidth` says a cluster is wide (2 cells) but the terminal renders it as multiple
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] narrow characters, your box drawing will be off.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - If the terminal honors mode 2027, then both your width calculation and the terminal’s rendering
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] agree.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Without mode 2027, older terminals may render `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦` as four separate emoji, each taking 2
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] cells (total 8), while your code says 2. That mismatch causes broken borders and misaligned text.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Should we enable it?
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] • Yes — it’s safe and helpful.* If the terminal doesn’t support mode 2027, it simply
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] ignores the sequence (DEC private modes are designed to be ignored by terminals that don’t
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] implement them). So there’s no downside, only a potential improvement.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Add it to your `bootTerminal()` sequence:
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] export async function bootTerminal(withMouse: boolean) {
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (!running) {
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		running = true;
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		Deno.stdin.setRaw(true);
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(GRAPHEME_MODE_ON)); // << add
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		inputTask = runInputTask(withMouse);
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		await sleep(20);
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	}
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// ...
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] }
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] And reset it in `haltTerminal()`:
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] export async function haltTerminal() {
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (!stopped) {
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		killMouse();
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(GRAPHEME_MODE_OFF)); // << add
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		Deno.stdin.setRaw(false);
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		writeConsole(resetConsole + enableCursor);
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 		// ...
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	}
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] }
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] A caveat: detection is not trivial
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] There’s no standard query to ask a terminal “do you support mode 2027?”. You could send a
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] test grapheme cluster and measure the cursor position, but that’s overkill for a prototype. Simply
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] enabling the mode and trusting your `Intl.Segmenter`-based `graphemeWidth()` is the pragmatic path.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] • Bottom line:* Use mode 2027 as a best‑effort improvement. Keep your width heuristics as a
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] fallback for terminals that don’t support it. That way your ANSI desktop manager will render
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] correctly on both modern terminals and older ones.
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Let me know if you want to integrate the escape sequences into `terminal.ts` and I can show the
2026-08-26 16:17:12 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] exact patch.
