2026-08-26 16:19:26 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] • Why include `ansiGraphemeModeOn/Off`?*
2026-08-26 16:19:26 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] As we discussed, mode `2027` tells the terminal to treat grapheme clusters as single units.
2026-08-26 16:19:26 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Enabling it at boot and disabling it on shutdown ensures your `stringWidth()` and the terminal’s
2026-08-26 16:19:27 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] rendering stay in sync. Even if a terminal doesn’t support it, the sequence is harmlessly ignored.
2026-08-26 16:19:27 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:19:27 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] • Note:* The mouse‑enabling string in `runInputTask` originally included `ansiCursorHide`,
2026-08-26 16:19:27 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] which is correct — you want the cursor hidden while mouse‑tracking is active. `killMouse` restores
2026-08-26 16:19:27 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] the cursor. The `haltTerminal` already writes a cursor‑show sequence, but now it uses the constant.
2026-08-26 16:19:27 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
