2026-08-26 16:23:58 [nitro@ryzen5] is there a way to determine support? how does cursor reporting work?
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.017 118.6KB 13.93s
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] There’s no standard query that tells you directly whether a terminal supports mode 2027.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The escape sequence itself is a DEC private mode, and terminals are free to ignore it. However, you
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] can infer support using cursor position reporting (CPR).
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] How cursor position reporting works
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The terminal supports a DSR (Device Status Report) request:
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] ESC [ 6 n
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] When it receives this, the terminal responds with a CPR (Cursor Position Report) of the form:
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] ESC [ row ; column R
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] So if you send `\x1b[6n` and then read the response from stdin, you get the current cursor row and
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] column (1‑based). This is how many terminal UIs detect window size or verify terminal capabilities.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Using CPR to test mode 2027 (theory)
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The idea is:
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 1. Move cursor to a known column (use absolute positioning: `ESC [ row ; col H`).
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 2. Send a test grapheme cluster (e.g., `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦`).
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 3. Send `ESC [6n`.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 4. Read the response and see how many columns the cursor advanced.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - If the terminal supports mode 2027 and renders the cluster as one wide glyph, the
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] cursor advances by 2 columns.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - If the terminal ignores mode 2027 and renders it as multiple separate emoji, the
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] cursor advances by more than 2 (e.g., 8).
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - If the terminal treats it as zero‑width or collapses it somehow, the result is unpredictable.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] This test is fragile because:
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - You need to read the async CPR response from stdin within a timeout.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - The terminal may wrap the cursor at the right edge, so you must place the cursor far enough left.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Some terminals don’t support CPR at all (rare, but possible).
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - The grapheme rendering can also depend on the font — even with mode 2027, a terminal could render
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] a ZWJ sequence as multiple glyphs if the font/locale says so.
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Is it worth doing?
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] For a desktop manager prototype, probably not. It adds complexity, race conditions, and
2026-08-26 16:24:13 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] edge cases. Mode 2027 is harmless if ignored, so the pragmatic path is:
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Always send `ESC[?2027h` at boot.
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Always send `ESC[?2027l` at shutdown.
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Use `Intl.Segmenter` to split graphemes and estimate width with your heuristic.
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Accept that on some terminals the width might be wrong, and adjust if needed (e.g., add a config
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] flag: `EMOJI_SEQUENCE_WIDTH = 2` vs. `8`).
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] If you later find a terminal that misbehaves, you can implement a one‑time detection routine that
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] runs at startup and adjusts the width constant accordingly. That would be a nice touch, but it’s
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] not necessary to get started.
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] A minimal detection sketch (if you want it later)
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] async function detectGraphemeSupport(): Promise<number> {
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Move to column 1, row 1
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode("\x1b[1;1H"));
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Clear line just in case
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode("\x1b[2K"));
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Send test cluster
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode("👨‍👩‍👧‍👦"));
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Query cursor
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode("\x1b[6n"));
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	// Read response from stdin with timeout
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	const response = await readWithTimeout(100);
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	const match = response.match(/\x1b\[(\d+);(\d+)R/);
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	if (!match) return 2; // default assumption
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	const col = parseInt(match[2], 10);
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 	return col; // 1‑based column after the cluster, so width = col - 1
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] }
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] But again, that’s a project for later.
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] • Bottom line:* Enable mode 2027, keep your width calculations, and don’t over‑engineer
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] detection right now. The terminal landscape is messy, and modern terminals (Windows Terminal,
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] iTerm2, kitty, etc.) generally do the right thing when you send the mode.
2026-08-26 16:24:14 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
