2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.1 deepseek-v4-flash-vision 🐋 🪠 0.7° $0.022 146.9KB 19.51s
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Thanks for sharing the updated files. A few things stand out — and one explains precisely why
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `GRAPHEME_DISABLE` is being ignored.
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 1. You’re sending disable, not enable
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] In `bootTerminal()` you wrote:
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Deno.stdout.writeSync(encoder.encode(GRAPHEME_DISABLE));
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] That sends `\x1b[?2027l`. If you want the terminal to enable grapheme cluster handling, you
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] should be sending `GRAPHEME_ENABLE` (`\x1b[?2027h`). So the current behavior is exactly what you’d
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] expect: you’re asking the terminal to turn off the mode, and if the terminal doesn’t
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] support it, it ignores the request.
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] If you actually want to test whether the terminal supports mode 2027, you can try sending
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] `GRAPHEME_ENABLE` instead. But even then, many terminals simply don’t implement this DEC private
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] mode, and they’ll silently ignore it. That’s not a bug in your code — it’s a limitation of the
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] terminal.
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 2. How to determine support? DECRQM vs. CPR
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] You already added `QUERY_2027` but never used it. That’s the DECRQM (Device Request Mode)
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] query:
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] \x1b[?2027$p
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:43 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] If the terminal supports DECRQM, it will respond with something like:
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] \x1b[?2027;0$y   // mode not recognized
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] \x1b[?2027;1$y   // mode set
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] \x1b[?2027;2$y   // mode reset
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] You can read that from stdin and parse. However, DECRQM itself is not universally implemented, so
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] absence of a response doesn’t necessarily mean mode 2027 is unsupported.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The cursor position report (CPR) approach is more universal but messier:
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Move cursor to column 1.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Write the test cluster `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦`.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Send `\x1b[6n` (DSR – request cursor position).
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Read the response `ESC [ row ; col R`.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - If the cursor advanced by 2 columns, the terminal likely treated the cluster as one wide glyph.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] If it advanced by 8 (or something else), it did not.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] This works on almost any ANSI terminal, but requires asynchronous read with timeout and careful
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] handling of partial input. It’s doable, but probably overkill for your current stage.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 3. A more fundamental issue: your width calc is still naive
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] Regardless of what the terminal does, `graphemeWidth()` currently does:
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] const codePoint = cluster.codePointAt(0)!
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] For a cluster like `👨‍👩‍👧‍👦`, that only examines the first code point and returns 2. But if the
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] terminal renders it as four separate emoji (each 2 cells), your `stringWidth` will be wrong —
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] you’ll calculate 2 cells when the actual output is 8 cells. That will break your layout.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] The whole point of mode 2027 is to make the terminal agree with your width calculation. If
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] the terminal ignores the mode, your calculations must match the actual rendering. Since you
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] can’t control the terminal, the robust approach is to use `Intl.Segmenter` to split into true
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] grapheme clusters, then compute width based on the entire cluster (not just the first code point).
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] We discussed this earlier; the code hasn’t been updated yet.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] So the next step is to replace `graphemeWidth` with a rasterizer that:
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 1. Uses `Intl.Segmenter` to iterate clusters.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 2. For each cluster, checks if it contains ZWJ (`\u200D`) or multiple wide code points, and treats
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] it as 2 cells.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 3. Falls back to per‑code‑point summing otherwise.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] This won’t perfectly match every terminal, but it gives you a sane baseline. If a terminal
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] misbehaves, you can tweak one constant.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 4. Suggestions
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] 
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Change `bootTerminal()` to send `GRAPHEME_ENABLE` instead of `GRAPHEME_DISABLE` – even if
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] ignored, it’s harmless and might help on supporting terminals.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Keep `QUERY_2027` for now; you can implement a DECRQM test later if you want.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Upgrade `graphemeWidth` to use `Intl.Segmenter` – this is the real fix for layout correctness.
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] - Watch out for the `native/` import path: `gadget.ts` imports from `./native/terminal.ts`, which
2026-08-26 16:45:44 [deepseek-v4-flash-vision] matches your file structure, so that’s fine.
