2026-08-27 15:26:30 [nitro@ryzen5] is Date getHours 1 based in js?
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.000 493B 13.46s
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] No — `getHours()` is zero-based. It returns a number from 0 to 23, where midnight is 0, noon is 12,
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] and 11 PM is 23.
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] Worth noting the same applies to `getMinutes()`, `getSeconds()`, and `getMilliseconds()`. The
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] commonly confused ones are:
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] - `getMonth()` — also zero-based (0 = January through 11 = December)
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] - `getDate()` — one-based (1 to 31)
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] - `getDay()` — zero-based day of week (0 = Sunday through 6 = Saturday)
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] If you need UTC instead of local time, `getUTCHours()` follows the same 0–23 convention.
2026-08-27 15:26:44 [glm-5.3-flash] 
