2026-08-27 15:48:50 [nitro@ryzen5] what year was r3000 processor released?
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 32.25KB 8.67s
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] The MIPS R3000 was released in 1988 by MIPS Computer Systems. It was a 32-bit RISC design
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] implementing the MIPS I instruction set, following the earlier R2000.
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] It saw wide adoption in late-80s/early-90s workstations — Silicon Graphics and DEC both built
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] machines around it — and its R3000A variant is probably best remembered as the main CPU inside the
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] original Sony PlayStation in 1994.
2026-08-27 15:49:00 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:50:06 [nitro@ryzen5] and the motorola 68000?
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 32.85KB 9.14s
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] The Motorola 68000 was released in 1979 (with systems using it shipping from 1980 onward).
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] It occupied an interesting middle ground: a 32-bit architecture internally — 32-bit registers and
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] flat address space design — but with a 16-bit external data bus and 24-bit addressing, which is why
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] it's often described as 16/32-bit. It drove roughly 1 million transistors... actually closer to
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 68,000, one of the largest chips of its era, built on an HMOS process running at 4–8 MHz initially.
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] Its adoption list is legendary: the original Apple Macintosh, Commodore Amiga, Atari ST, Sega Mega
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] Drive/Genesis, and countless Sun, HP, and Silicon Graphics workstations — making it the dominant
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] CPU of late-80s personal computing before Intel's x86 took the mainstream.
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] Fun contrast with your earlier question: the 68000 predates the R3000 by about nine years, and MIPS
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] machines like the SGI Indigo eventually displaced 68k-based workstations in the early 90s.
2026-08-27 15:50:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 
