2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.001 53.33KB 24.35s
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] `uint` isn't a standard C/C++ type — it's a BSD/POSIX typedef for `unsigned int`, defined in
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] `<sys/types.h>` (which newlib provides on the Pico). The SDK headers use it everywhere and pull it
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] in transitively, so once `hardware/gpio.h` is included it's available.
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] Two things to watch in `picomain.cpp` specifically:
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] - Your `gpio` declaration sits at the very top of the file, before `#include
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] <hardware/pio.h>` and friends. Using `uint` there needs an explicit `#include <sys/types.h>` at the
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] top, or the declaration moved below the SDK includes.
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] - `uint` won't exist on host builds or MSVC, so if this file ever compiles off-device, `unsigned
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] int` is the safer spelling.
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] Simplest option that avoids all of that: keep the array of plain literals —
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] const int gpio[]={0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,14,15,16,17,18,19,20,21,22,26,27};
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] `int` converts to the SDK's `uint` parameters implicitly with no warnings for these values, and no
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] extra include is needed.
2026-08-28 22:18:13 [glm-5.3-flash] 
