2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.002 78.62KB 28.07s
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] Yes — `gpio_get_all()` in `hardware/gpio.h` reads the whole bank in one register access, returning
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] a bitmask of pins 0–29 (RP2040) or 0–31 (RP2350):
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] uint32_t all = gpio_get_all();
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] if(all & (1u<<KEY0_PIN)) { ... }
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] Because it's a single SIO register read, every pin is sampled at the same instant — unlike four
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] separate `gpio_get()` calls, which can straddle a pin change. That matters for anything where the
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] pins are related, like your key/fire groups.
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] For your `keys` byte specifically, if KEY0–KEY3 are contiguous pins, it's a mask and shift:
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] uint32_t readKeys(){
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 	return (gpio_get_all() >> KEY0_PIN) & 0xF;
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] If the pins aren't contiguous, one read plus a gather is still atomic:
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] uint32_t readGpioGroup(const uint *pins, uint pinCount){
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 	uint32_t all=gpio_get_all();
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 	uint32_t result=0;
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 	for(uint i=0;i<pinCount;i++){
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 		if(all & (1u<<pins[i])) result|=(1u<<i);
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 	}
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 	return result;
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] Two things to note for your wiring:
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] - Your buttons are active-low (pull-ups, pressed = 0), and your current `fire`/`keys` code treats a
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] high as set — so a one-shot `uint32_t pressed = ~gpio_get_all();` followed by bit tests would fix
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] the inversion and collapse both reads into a single register access.
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] - The output side has the same family: `gpio_put_masked(mask, value)`, `gpio_set_mask()`,
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] `gpio_clr_mask()`, `gpio_set_dir_masked()` — handy for the LED/palette-style writes.
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] On RP2350, pins 32–47 live in a second register (`gpio_in_hi`), but `gpio_get_all()` handles both
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] banks for you, so there's no reason to touch the registers directly.
2026-08-28 22:35:58 [glm-5.3-flash] 
