2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] No — there's no explicit UART pin setup left in this file. Nothing calls `uart_init()` or
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] `gpio_set_function(..., GPIO_FUNC_UART)` anywhere.
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] Two indirect things still touch UART territory, though:
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 1. `stdio_init_all()` (first line of `picoInstance()`) will configure the default UART pins
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] (GPIO0/GPIO1 on an RP2040) if your build has `pico_enable_stdio_uart` enabled in CMake.
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] With `USE_CDC` defined and the chars-available callback wired up, you're presumably UART-free in
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] the build config, but that's decided outside this file.
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 2. The `USE_ROM` GPIO loop includes pins 0 and 1 in its list:
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash]    `{0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7,14,15,16,17,18,19,20,21,22,26,27}`
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash]    `gpio_init()` resets a pin to SIO function, so if `stdio_init_all()` did set up UART on
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 0/1, this loop would strip the UART function off them again. Any subsequent `log()`/`printf` output
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] via UART would go nowhere after that point.
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] So the practical answer: if UART stdio is disabled in CMake, nothing sets UART pins and you're
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] clean. If it's enabled, pins 0 and 1 get configured by `stdio_init_all()` and then immediately
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] clobbered by the ROM input init — so you'd want to drop them from that vector or re-apply the UART
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] function afterwards.
2026-08-28 22:45:25 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 22:45:26 [glm-5.3-flash] One other small thing I noticed while checking: pins 16–22 in that same ROM list overlap with your
2026-08-28 22:45:26 [glm-5.3-flash] touch controller (`TP_CS_PIN`/`TP_IRQ_PIN` on 16/17) and the SD pins (18–22) you define further
2026-08-28 22:45:26 [glm-5.3-flash] down, so those will also fight each other if both paths are ever active.
2026-08-28 22:45:26 [glm-5.3-flash] 
