2026-08-28 23:19:32 [nitro@ryzen5] how do i clear pullup
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 🟠nitrologic Relay 2.0.2 glm-5.3-flash Z 🪠 0.7° $0.002 68.13KB 63.38s
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] Two ways, both in `hardware/gpio.h`:
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] gpio_set_pulls(pin, false, false);	// explicit: clears both up and down
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] gpio_disable_pulls(pin);		// same thing, inline wrapper in newer SDKs
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] Two gotchas worth knowing:
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 1. `gpio_pull_down(pin)` is not "clear" — it swaps pull-up for pull-down. And RP2040 pads
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] come out of chip reset with pull-downs enabled, so to get a genuinely floating input you
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] need to clear both, which is what the calls above do.
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 2. `gpio_init()` and `gpio_set_function()` don't touch pulls. They only change the function
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] select in the IO bank; the pad settings (pulls, drive strength, hysteresis) persist. So a pull-up
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] set once stays set across re-init.
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] That second point matters for your code: `initTouch()` sets a pull-up on TP_IRQ (GP17), and the
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] `USE_ROM` loop later does `gpio_init(17)` — the pull-up survives that. If you want the ROM pins
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] truly floating, the loop needs to clear them explicitly:
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] for(size_t i=0;i<gpio.size();i++){
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 	uint pin=gpio[i];
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 	gpio_init(pin);
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 	gpio_set_dir(pin,GPIO_IN);
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 	gpio_set_pulls(pin,false,false);
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] }
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] Conversely, the same persistence is what makes your button init work — `gpio_pull_up()` in the
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] `#else` branch doesn't need re-applying each loop.
2026-08-28 23:20:37 [glm-5.3-flash] 
