2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] Thanks — this one's new to me. It's a Godot 4 diagnostics node that bridges local WebSocket clients
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] (TCP listener on 8080, promoted to `WebSocketPeer` via `accept_stream`) to a single upstream master
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] at `ws://localhost:9000`, with an on-screen `TextEdit` log and an echo handler. A few observations:
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] • Likely bug — the 1K frame counter never fires.* `count&1024==1` can never be true: `count
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] & 1024` only ever yields 0 or 1024, never 1. (Precedence isn't the issue here — `&` binds tighter
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] than `==` in GDScript, so it groups as written.) A modulo check would match the intent.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] • `sumDelta` is cumulative.* It reports total elapsed time since startup, not per-1K-frame
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] timing. If it's meant as a frame-time diagnostic, divide by 1024 at report time or reset after each
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] report.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] • No upstream recovery.* Once `masterSocket` reaches `STATE_CLOSED`, nothing reconnects it
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] — `poll()` on a closed peer is inert, so a dropped or failed handshake is permanent for the
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] session. Relatedly, the commented-out `set_process(false)` in `_ready` wouldn't have guarded
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] anything: `connect_to_url` returning `OK` only means the handshake started, and failures surface
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] later in `poll`.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] • Log growth will dominate at scale.* `log_view.text += ...` per packet is O(n) per append,
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] which will become the bottleneck well before the networking does. For a many-to-many load test,
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] batching, a capped ring buffer, or throttled flushing would be worth considering.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] • Topology note.* Despite the "many to many" header, upstream is a single peer. True
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] many-to-many would need a pool of upstream peers managed like the local `sockets` array.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] • Positives:* the reverse iteration with `remove_at` during cleanup is correctly done, and
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] the accept path is sound.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] Minor style points: `@onready` on a `TextEdit.new()` is redundant, `onPacket`'s parameters could be
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] typed, and semicolon/spacing use is inconsistent (`"Received"+message` is missing a space).
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] Happy to sketch a reconnect routine or a capped log approach if you'd like.
2026-08-29 12:06:40 [glm-5.3-flash] 
