2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] This is a new script. It provides a clean, straightforward continuous rotation for a 3D object
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] using exported speed properties.
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] A couple of technical observations regarding how Godot handles rotations:
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Local vs. Global Rotation: Calling `rotate_y()` and `rotate_x()` applies transformations
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] around the node's local coordinate axes in sequence every frame. Because rotating around the local
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] X-axis tilts the local Y-axis (and vice versa), the object will eventually exhibit a continuous
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] tumbling precession rather than a fixed-axis turntable rotation. If an independent yaw/pitch or
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] turntable effect is preferred, rotating around the global Y-axis while pitching on the local X-axis
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] is often the standard pattern.
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Unused Boilerplate: The default `_ready()` callback with the placeholder comment is
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] unused and can be removed cleanly if initialization logic is not required.
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] Please let me know if you would like any specific adjustments or code examples for different
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] rotation behaviors.
2026-08-29 12:08:59 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
