2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] Yes, absolutely. In Godot, a `Node3D` doesn't render geometry directly, but you can give it a large
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] cube using either of these standard approaches:
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 1. In the Godot Editor
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Add a `MeshInstance3D` as a child of your `Node3D` (or attach your script directly to a
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] `MeshInstance3D`).
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] • In the Inspector for the `MeshInstance3D`, assign a new `BoxMesh` to the `Mesh` property.
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] • Expand the `BoxMesh` settings and increase the `Size` property (for example, `Vector3(10,
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 10, 10)` for a 10-meter cube).
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 2. Dynamically via GDScript
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] If you prefer creating and configuring the cube entirely in code, you can instantiate a
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] `MeshInstance3D` with a `BoxMesh` in your `_ready()` function and set its dimensions to whatever
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] scale you need.
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] 
2026-08-29 12:11:37 [gemini-3.7-flash] Would you like the GDScript code to set up the mesh instance dynamically?
